{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q3LgLRuhtwU1a9UVYgSjmd","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration component PPV PPA transverse vertical longitudinal modelling attenuation literature\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X\"},{\"title\":\"PPV and Frequency Characteristics of Tunnel Blast‐Induced Vibrations on Tunnel Surfaces and Tunnel Entrance Slope Faces - Wang - 2021 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2021/5527115\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"1 NEW PREDICTION MODEL OF BLAST-INDUCED PPV BASED ON\",\"url\":\"https://geomatejournal.com/geomate/article/download/4024/3311/7921\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001\"},{\"title\":\"Blast Vibration Criteria Selection\",\"url\":\"https://www.eweb.org/documents/Projects/Water%20Storage/Blast-Vibration-Criteria-Selection.pdf\"}]\n\nI've found relevant literature on blast vibration components, PPV (peak particle velocity), PPA (peak particle acceleration), and attenuation modeling. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Blast Vibration Components and Measurement\n\nMonitoring systems record longitudinal (L), transverse (T) and vertical (V) components with electromagnetic geophones. Vibration data include peak particle velocity (PPV), peak particle acceleration (PPA), peak displacement, and frequency (f).\n\n## Directional Analysis\n\nVertical, longitudinal, and transversal data were analyzed to determine seismic wave attenuation, though the vertical component is often used since longitudinal and transversal components depend heavily on propagation direction. Center frequencies of longitudinal, transverse, and vertical velocity components vary, with examples showing 119 Hz, 124.8 Hz, and 122.8 Hz respectively.\n\n## Attenuation Modeling\n\nRecent research establishes PPV attenuation equations considering the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves in blasting vibration waves. The scaled distance (SD) relationship is the widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV.\n\nCommon empirical attenuation equations represent prediction models for PPV as a function of scaled distance, and attenuation is best studied with multiple stations and a single blast because the explosion source condition remains constant for related monitoring locations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"3e5d1096-f627-4dfb-8444-e1a1ab767cf6","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:25.531Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration component PPV PPA transverse vertical longitudinal modelling attenuation literature","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01HfZRGucM5bYYDjUTGAttAD","content":[{"title":"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X"},{"title":"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X"},{"title":"PPV and Frequency Characteristics of Tunnel Blast‐Induced Vibrations on Tunnel Surfaces and Tunnel Entrance Slope Faces - Wang - 2021 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2021/5527115"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"1 NEW PREDICTION MODEL OF BLAST-INDUCED PPV BASED ON","url":"https://geomatejournal.com/geomate/article/download/4024/3311/7921"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001"},{"title":"Blast Vibration Criteria Selection","url":"https://www.eweb.org/documents/Projects/Water%20Storage/Blast-Vibration-Criteria-Selection.pdf"}]},"I've found relevant literature on blast vibration components, PPV (peak particle velocity), PPA (peak particle acceleration), and attenuation modeling. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Blast Vibration Components and Measurement\n\nMonitoring systems record longitudinal (L), transverse (T) and vertical (V) components with electromagnetic geophones. Vibration data include peak particle velocity (PPV), peak particle acceleration (PPA), peak displacement, and frequency (f).\n\n## Directional Analysis\n\nVertical, longitudinal, and transversal data were analyzed to determine seismic wave attenuation, though the vertical component is often used since longitudinal and transversal components depend heavily on propagation direction. Center frequencies of longitudinal, transverse, and vertical velocity components vary, with examples showing 119 Hz, 124.8 Hz, and 122.8 Hz respectively.\n\n## Attenuation Modeling\n\nRecent research establishes PPV attenuation equations considering the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves in blasting vibration waves. The scaled distance (SD) relationship is the widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV.\n\nCommon empirical attenuation equations represent prediction models for PPV as a function of scaled distance, and attenuation is best studied with multiple stations and a single blast because the explosion source condition remains constant for related monitoring locations."],"durationSeconds":7.378661290999997,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QfdQ9eTnTxrv9otLNU4HBV","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration multivariate response prediction correlated components scaled distance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using multivariate\",\"url\":\"https://www.ias.ac.in/public/Volumes/sadh/049/00/0141.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of capability of blast-induced ground vibration predictors considering measurement distance and different error measures | Environmental Earth Sciences\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-019-8427-5\"},{\"title\":\"Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775518302476\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation and Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) Approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-6489/3/4/36\"},{\"title\":\"Advancing the Prediction and Evaluation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Deep Ensemble Learning with Uncertainty Assessment\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/5/182\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using artificial neural network - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160909000574\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Blast Induced Vibration Estimation by Using Different Machine Learning Methods in a Tunnel Excavation\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/86112352/Blast_Induced_Vibration_Estimation_by_Using_Different_Machine_Learning_Methods_in_a_Tunnel_Excavation\"}]\n\nI've found relevant research on blast vibration prediction using multivariate approaches and scaled distance methods. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview of Blast Vibration Prediction\n\nThe blast-induced ground vibration prediction using scaled distance regression analysis is one of the most popular methods employed by engineers for many decades, using the maximum charge per delay and distance of monitoring as the major factors for predicting the peak particle velocity (PPV).\n\n## Multivariate Analysis Approaches\n\nMultivariate equations containing blast design parameters can be created by stepwise regression. Researchers have compared different prediction methodologies, and studies comparing artificial neural networks with USBM predictor (cube root scaled distance) and multivariate regression analysis equations found better results by ANN over USBM and MVRA prediction.\n\n## Considerations for Multi-hole Blasting\n\nDuring production blasting, waves induced by blasting of different holes can interfere destructively with each other, which may result in higher PPV than the predicted value with scaled distance regression analysis—a phenomenon that is not considered while using scaled distance regression analysis.\n\n## Model Variables\n\nVariables commonly considered in blast vibration prediction include the number of blast holes, ratio of burden to hole diameter, ratio of bench height to burden, maximum instantaneous charge, scaled distance, spacing, and burden.\n\nThe research shows that while traditional scaled distance methods remain effective, multivariate approaches and machine learning techniques can provide enhanced predictions, particularly when accounting for the complex interactions between multiple blast holes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"ca6c5586-ceb8-4bca-9fa4-e511e35a83a5","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:26.539Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration multivariate response prediction correlated components scaled distance","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_015vgUuQFVJaWgEQPqaYieEL","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting"},{"title":"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using multivariate","url":"https://www.ias.ac.in/public/Volumes/sadh/049/00/0141.pdf"},{"title":"Evaluation of capability of blast-induced ground vibration predictors considering measurement distance and different error measures | Environmental Earth Sciences","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-019-8427-5"},{"title":"Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775518302476"},{"title":"Evaluation and Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) Approach","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-6489/3/4/36"},{"title":"Advancing the Prediction and Evaluation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Deep Ensemble Learning with Uncertainty Assessment","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/5/182"},{"title":"Prediction of blast-induced ground vibration using artificial neural network - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160909000574"},{"title":"(PDF) Blast Induced Vibration Estimation by Using Different Machine Learning Methods in a Tunnel Excavation","url":"https://www.academia.edu/86112352/Blast_Induced_Vibration_Estimation_by_Using_Different_Machine_Learning_Methods_in_a_Tunnel_Excavation"}]},"I've found relevant research on blast vibration prediction using multivariate approaches and scaled distance methods. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Overview of Blast Vibration Prediction\n\nThe blast-induced ground vibration prediction using scaled distance regression analysis is one of the most popular methods employed by engineers for many decades, using the maximum charge per delay and distance of monitoring as the major factors for predicting the peak particle velocity (PPV).\n\n## Multivariate Analysis Approaches\n\nMultivariate equations containing blast design parameters can be created by stepwise regression. Researchers have compared different prediction methodologies, and studies comparing artificial neural networks with USBM predictor (cube root scaled distance) and multivariate regression analysis equations found better results by ANN over USBM and MVRA prediction.\n\n## Considerations for Multi-hole Blasting\n\nDuring production blasting, waves induced by blasting of different holes can interfere destructively with each other, which may result in higher PPV than the predicted value with scaled distance regression analysis—a phenomenon that is not considered while using scaled distance regression analysis.\n\n## Model Variables\n\nVariables commonly considered in blast vibration prediction include the number of blast holes, ratio of burden to hole diameter, ratio of bench height to burden, maximum instantaneous charge, scaled distance, spacing, and burden.\n\nThe research shows that while traditional scaled distance methods remain effective, multivariate approaches and machine learning techniques can provide enhanced predictions, particularly when accounting for the complex interactions between multiple blast holes."],"durationSeconds":7.819615250000003,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BQehzY2Vec6dwou11akCfX","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration peak scaling limitations physics-based source model vs empirical attenuation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"Modelling the Source of Blasting for the Numerical Simulation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Review | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/308665913_Modelling_the_Source_of_Blasting_for_the_Numerical_Simulation_of_Blast-Induced_Ground_Vibrations_A_Review\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-00640-9\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Expansion of Blast Vibration Attenuation Equations for Deeply Buried Small Clearance Tunnels Based on Dimensional Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.889504/full\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8548544/\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"}]\n\nI've retrieved search results on blast vibration peak scaling and the limitations of empirical versus physics-based approaches. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Empirical Attenuation Models\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict peak particle velocity (PPV) is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, and various empirical equations have been developed by researchers. Different scaling laws have been observed, with some research showing cube root scaling for explosive quantity, while other experiments found square root scaling depending on the conditions.\n\n## Limitations of Traditional Empirical Approaches\n\nWhile traditional empirical models are simple and quick to calculate, their prediction accuracy is limited due to oversimplification of influencing factors, and they are generally applicable only under specific site conditions, often failing to account for various factors in complex terrains.\n\nTraditional empirical formulas do not reflect the effects of explosive properties, explosive type, charging method, hole size, and surrounding rock parameters on blast vibration velocity.\n\n## Additional Complexity Factors\n\nBlast vibration attenuation between 2 m and 4 m depends not only on frequency but also on amplitude. While the proportional dose is the main factor of blasting vibration velocity, the peak mass vibration velocity is also inversely related to the free surface area.\n\nThe search results show that empirical models struggle with site-specific variability and multiple influencing parameters, leading researchers to develop more sophisticated approaches including neural networks and numerical simulations that better account for complex physical factors.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"a34bef13-de99-4bd7-8b9e-ea31d796d08d","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:27.246Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration peak scaling limitations physics-based source model vs empirical attenuation","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FLA615YncYkhEWmavdBHw4","content":[{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"Modelling the Source of Blasting for the Numerical Simulation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Review | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/308665913_Modelling_the_Source_of_Blasting_for_the_Numerical_Simulation_of_Blast-Induced_Ground_Vibrations_A_Review"},{"title":"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-021-00640-9"},{"title":"Frontiers | Expansion of Blast Vibration Attenuation Equations for Deeply Buried Small Clearance Tunnels Based on Dimensional Analysis","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.889504/full"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8548544/"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"}]},"I've retrieved search results on blast vibration peak scaling and the limitations of empirical versus physics-based approaches. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Empirical Attenuation Models\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict peak particle velocity (PPV) is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, and various empirical equations have been developed by researchers. Different scaling laws have been observed, with some research showing cube root scaling for explosive quantity, while other experiments found square root scaling depending on the conditions.\n\n## Limitations of Traditional Empirical Approaches\n\nWhile traditional empirical models are simple and quick to calculate, their prediction accuracy is limited due to oversimplification of influencing factors, and they are generally applicable only under specific site conditions, often failing to account for various factors in complex terrains.\n\nTraditional empirical formulas do not reflect the effects of explosive properties, explosive type, charging method, hole size, and surrounding rock parameters on blast vibration velocity.\n\n## Additional Complexity Factors\n\nBlast vibration attenuation between 2 m and 4 m depends not only on frequency but also on amplitude. While the proportional dose is the main factor of blasting vibration velocity, the peak mass vibration velocity is also inversely related to the free surface area.\n\nThe search results show that empirical models struggle with site-specific variability and multiple influencing parameters, leading researchers to develop more sophisticated approaches including neural networks and numerical simulations that better account for complex physical factors."],"durationSeconds":7.294887583000003,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014VCvSN2uD1ZvZSVxNrMxYk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration waveform scaling scenario vs spectral matching synthetic simulation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Engineering Safety-Oriented Blasting-Induced Seismic Wave Signal Processing: An EMD Endpoint Suppression Method Based on Multi-Scale Feature - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12252125/\"},{\"title\":\"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023\"},{\"title\":\"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547\"},{\"title\":\"Large-Scale 3D Ground-Motion Synthesis with Physics-Inspired Latent Operator Flow Matching\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.17403\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Simulation of Blasting Induced Ground Vibration by Using Artificial Neural Network\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/265144688_Simulation_of_Blasting_Induced_Ground_Vibration_by_Using_Artificial_Neural_Network\"},{\"title\":\"blast vibration intensity: Topics by Science.gov\",\"url\":\"https://www.science.gov/topicpages/b/blast+vibration+intensity.html\"},{\"title\":\"Simulation of blast-induced ground vibrations using a machine learning-assisted mechanical framework | Environmental Earth Sciences | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-023-11194-6\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration waveform scaling scenario versus spectral matching synthetic simulation:\n\n## Key Approaches to Blast Vibration Simulation\n\n**Waveform Scaling Scenarios:**\nGround vibration prediction methods focus on seed waveform and charge weight scaling techniques. The traditional Anderson linear superposition method can achieve complete vibration waveform prediction based on single experimental data and can predict responses under varying excitation intensities (charge weights) and complex timing sequences (multi-hole delays).\n\n**Spectral Matching and Synthetic Simulation:**\nThe common time-domain superposition method has limitations in accurately controlling spectrum characteristics, and a frequency-domain amplitude-phase joint waveform simulation method based on Fourier analysis has been proposed to meet blasting vibration analysis and safety assessment needs. \n\nCompared to the time-domain superposition method, the frequency-domain magnitude-phase joint waveform simulation method can more accurately characterize the distribution and variation of each frequency component, providing more accurate input loads for fine analysis of dynamic response of complex structures.\n\n**Waveform Matching Benefits:**\nWaveform matching maintains the unique shape characteristics of blasting-induced seismic waves, allowing decomposed components to better reflect the complex dynamics of the waves.\n\nThe research indicates that frequency-domain spectral matching approaches offer improved accuracy in controlling spectrum characteristics compared to traditional time-domain waveform scaling methods, particularly for complex structural analysis applications.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"57fd225e-a517-4699-bf45-36623bc19ebe","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:27.456Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration waveform scaling scenario vs spectral matching synthetic simulation","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01268xCUfe7ubt3rn5ZnuK9f","content":[{"title":"Engineering Safety-Oriented Blasting-Induced Seismic Wave Signal Processing: An EMD Endpoint Suppression Method Based on Multi-Scale Feature - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12252125/"},{"title":"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023"},{"title":"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017"},{"title":"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547"},{"title":"Large-Scale 3D Ground-Motion Synthesis with Physics-Inspired Latent Operator Flow Matching","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.17403"},{"title":"(PDF) Simulation of Blasting Induced Ground Vibration by Using Artificial Neural Network","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/265144688_Simulation_of_Blasting_Induced_Ground_Vibration_by_Using_Artificial_Neural_Network"},{"title":"blast vibration intensity: Topics by Science.gov","url":"https://www.science.gov/topicpages/b/blast+vibration+intensity.html"},{"title":"Simulation of blast-induced ground vibrations using a machine learning-assisted mechanical framework | Environmental Earth Sciences | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s12665-023-11194-6"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration waveform scaling scenario versus spectral matching synthetic simulation:\n\n## Key Approaches to Blast Vibration Simulation\n\n**Waveform Scaling Scenarios:**\nGround vibration prediction methods focus on seed waveform and charge weight scaling techniques. The traditional Anderson linear superposition method can achieve complete vibration waveform prediction based on single experimental data and can predict responses under varying excitation intensities (charge weights) and complex timing sequences (multi-hole delays).\n\n**Spectral Matching and Synthetic Simulation:**\nThe common time-domain superposition method has limitations in accurately controlling spectrum characteristics, and a frequency-domain amplitude-phase joint waveform simulation method based on Fourier analysis has been proposed to meet blasting vibration analysis and safety assessment needs. \n\nCompared to the time-domain superposition method, the frequency-domain magnitude-phase joint waveform simulation method can more accurately characterize the distribution and variation of each frequency component, providing more accurate input loads for fine analysis of dynamic response of complex structures.\n\n**Waveform Matching Benefits:**\nWaveform matching maintains the unique shape characteristics of blasting-induced seismic waves, allowing decomposed components to better reflect the complex dynamics of the waves.\n\nThe research indicates that frequency-domain spectral matching approaches offer improved accuracy in controlling spectrum characteristics compared to traditional time-domain waveform scaling methods, particularly for complex structural analysis applications."],"durationSeconds":7.865385333999999,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013UyaEt33ovFH8AS5QgdKyK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration time history scaling component peak velocity acceleration recorded waveform\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Research on the Prediction of Blasting Vibration Velocity in Open Pit Mines Based on the SSA-BP Model - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12676330/\"},{\"title\":\"MANAGING THE IMPACTS OF BLAST-INDUCED VIBRATION AND OVERPRESSURE ON FISH\",\"url\":\"https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/clas/pdfs/fhwa-flh-td_19001.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Relationship between peak particle acceleration, velocity and displacement of blast vibration\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392403274_Relationship_between_peak_particle_acceleration_velocity_and_displacement_of_blast_vibration\"},{\"title\":\"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...\",\"url\":\"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Vibration velocity and frequency characteristics of surrounding rock of adjacent tunnel under blasting excavation | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-12203-7\"},{\"title\":\"Relationship between blasting operation and slope stability: a case study at Borneo Indo Bara open pit coal mine | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-81784-2\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"Seismic peak partcile velocity and acceleration response to mining faces   firing in a light of numerical modeling and underground measurements\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1903.04232\"},{\"title\":\"Modelling of blast vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0148906288909849\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about blast vibration time history scaling and its components:\n\n## Waveform Recording and Analysis\n\nBlast vibration waveforms are typically recorded using accelerometers in the field, and recorded particle acceleration waveforms are integrated to obtain particle velocity waveforms, which are then integrated into displacement waveforms.\n\n## Peak Parameters and Components\n\nVibration velocity waveforms are typically analyzed in the horizontal radial, horizontal tangential, and vertical directions, with peak vibration velocity and dominant vibration frequency of each waveform measured, and the maximum vibration velocities in the X, Y, and Z directions at each monitoring point being vector-synthesized.\n\n## Relationships Between Measures\n\nDisplacement and acceleration time histories are simply the integration and differentiation of the velocity time history, respectively, and the peak over time is reported. More specifically, a peak particle acceleration (PPA) is defined as the largest absolute particle acceleration in the waveform, while PPV or PPD is defined in a similar way from a particle velocity or displacement waveform.\n\n## Signature Hole Analysis\n\nVibration waveforms show the resulting velocity and acceleration versus time corresponding to a single blast event, and signature hole analysis uses a computer program to predict blast-induced vibration by superimposing recorded waveforms and inputting the time of detonation of each hole to predict peak particle velocity and blast vibration frequency.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"e0047a5c-e92d-450e-a418-91f4c2f72c5d","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:28.773Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration time history scaling component peak velocity acceleration recorded waveform","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Pg4QCQdbrXmvR8odxY5N4W","content":[{"title":"Research on the Prediction of Blasting Vibration Velocity in Open Pit Mines Based on the SSA-BP Model - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12676330/"},{"title":"MANAGING THE IMPACTS OF BLAST-INDUCED VIBRATION AND OVERPRESSURE ON FISH","url":"https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/clas/pdfs/fhwa-flh-td_19001.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Relationship between peak particle acceleration, velocity and displacement of blast vibration","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392403274_Relationship_between_peak_particle_acceleration_velocity_and_displacement_of_blast_vibration"},{"title":"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...","url":"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf"},{"title":"Vibration velocity and frequency characteristics of surrounding rock of adjacent tunnel under blasting excavation | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-022-12203-7"},{"title":"Relationship between blasting operation and slope stability: a case study at Borneo Indo Bara open pit coal mine | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-81784-2"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"Seismic peak partcile velocity and acceleration response to mining faces   firing in a light of numerical modeling and underground measurements","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1903.04232"},{"title":"Modelling of blast vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0148906288909849"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about blast vibration time history scaling and its components:\n\n## Waveform Recording and Analysis\n\nBlast vibration waveforms are typically recorded using accelerometers in the field, and recorded particle acceleration waveforms are integrated to obtain particle velocity waveforms, which are then integrated into displacement waveforms.\n\n## Peak Parameters and Components\n\nVibration velocity waveforms are typically analyzed in the horizontal radial, horizontal tangential, and vertical directions, with peak vibration velocity and dominant vibration frequency of each waveform measured, and the maximum vibration velocities in the X, Y, and Z directions at each monitoring point being vector-synthesized.\n\n## Relationships Between Measures\n\nDisplacement and acceleration time histories are simply the integration and differentiation of the velocity time history, respectively, and the peak over time is reported. More specifically, a peak particle acceleration (PPA) is defined as the largest absolute particle acceleration in the waveform, while PPV or PPD is defined in a similar way from a particle velocity or displacement waveform.\n\n## Signature Hole Analysis\n\nVibration waveforms show the resulting velocity and acceleration versus time corresponding to a single blast event, and signature hole analysis uses a computer program to predict blast-induced vibration by superimposing recorded waveforms and inputting the time of detonation of each hole to predict peak particle velocity and blast vibration frequency."],"durationSeconds":9.492201333999997,"searchCount":1}}
