{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LSaG4ZzFEEcSLKVMUWyoAt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration separate component attenuation \"transverse\" \"vertical\" \"longitudinal\" PPV regression predictor\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X\"},{\"title\":\"PPV and Frequency Characteristics of Tunnel Blast‐Induced Vibrations on Tunnel Surfaces and Tunnel Entrance Slope Faces - Wang - 2021 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library\",\"url\":\"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2021/5527115\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849\"},{\"title\":\"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"}]\n\nThe search has returned relevant academic articles on blast vibration attenuation. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Blast Vibration Components and PPV Attenuation\n\n**Separate Component Analysis:**\n\nData from three directions—vertical, longitudinal, and transversal—were analyzed to determine seismic wave attenuation, though the longitudinal and transversal components depend heavily on propagation direction.\n\nMonitoring systems recorded longitudinal (L), transverse (T) and vertical (V) components with electromagnetic geophones.\n\n**PPV Regression Predictors:**\n\nMaximum values of the three component velocities (where x, y, z represent peak component particle velocity) are used as prediction parameters.\n\nWith changes in rock mass properties, the site coefficients of blasting vibration prediction equations for PPV show varying trends.\n\n**Wave Component Attenuation:**\n\nA typical blasting vibration wave is a composite wave whose attenuation law is affected by the type of dominant wave component, and PPV attenuation equations can be established taking into account the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves.\n\nRegression analysis shows the longitudinal component has moderate to high coefficient of determination (R² ≈ 0.81), while the transversal component shows stronger correlation (R² ≈ 0.89).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"e6677278-c22c-4bbe-94e4-0cc2ce3c1986","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:44.865Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration separate component attenuation \"transverse\" \"vertical\" \"longitudinal\" PPV regression predictor","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_018LQy69SPMJq31RpiPye7ra","content":[{"title":"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X"},{"title":"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full"},{"title":"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X"},{"title":"PPV and Frequency Characteristics of Tunnel Blast‐Induced Vibrations on Tunnel Surfaces and Tunnel Entrance Slope Faces - Wang - 2021 - Shock and Vibration - Wiley Online Library","url":"https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1155/2021/5527115"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849"},{"title":"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001"},{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"}]},"The search has returned relevant academic articles on blast vibration attenuation. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Blast Vibration Components and PPV Attenuation\n\n**Separate Component Analysis:**\n\nData from three directions—vertical, longitudinal, and transversal—were analyzed to determine seismic wave attenuation, though the longitudinal and transversal components depend heavily on propagation direction.\n\nMonitoring systems recorded longitudinal (L), transverse (T) and vertical (V) components with electromagnetic geophones.\n\n**PPV Regression Predictors:**\n\nMaximum values of the three component velocities (where x, y, z represent peak component particle velocity) are used as prediction parameters.\n\nWith changes in rock mass properties, the site coefficients of blasting vibration prediction equations for PPV show varying trends.\n\n**Wave Component Attenuation:**\n\nA typical blasting vibration wave is a composite wave whose attenuation law is affected by the type of dominant wave component, and PPV attenuation equations can be established taking into account the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves.\n\nRegression analysis shows the longitudinal component has moderate to high coefficient of determination (R² ≈ 0.81), while the transversal component shows stronger correlation (R² ≈ 0.89)."],"durationSeconds":7.131588625000004,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013kszuwLQTMskPZsKg9s5FN","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration \"vector sum\" \"resultant\" vs component PPV peak particle velocity comparison\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...\",\"url\":\"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Interpreting a blast report Printed: January 4, 2021 (V 10.72 - 10.72.1)\",\"url\":\"https://ballaratgoldmine.com.au/wp-content/uploads/Interpreting-a-Blast-Report.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Resultant Peak Particle Velocity against Scaled Distance for the Charge... | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Resultant-Peak-Particle-Velocity-against-Scaled-Distance-for-the-Charge-Sizes_fig1_274008170\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Relationship between peak particle acceleration, velocity and displacement of blast vibration\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392403274_Relationship_between_peak_particle_acceleration_velocity_and_displacement_of_blast_vibration\"},{\"title\":\"Vibration and air pressure monitoring of seismic sources\",\"url\":\"https://www.crewes.org/Documents/ResearchReports/2007/2007-16.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Vector Sum and PPV - Texcel\",\"url\":\"https://texcel.com.au/vector-sum-and-ppv/\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis of the Impact of ground vibration on the blast zone within\",\"url\":\"https://www.bio-conferences.org/articles/bioconf/pdf/2024/65/bioconf_btmic2024_01067.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Estimation of near-field peak particle velocity\",\"url\":\"https://academicjournals.org/article/article1379508799_Arora%20and%20Dey.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"},{\"title\":\"Peak particle velocity definition (From Instantel, 2001). | Download Scientific Diagram\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Peak-particle-velocity-definition-From-Instantel-2001_fig1_265347764\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comparison of vector sum (resultant) versus component approaches for blast vibration PPV measurement:\n\n## Key Definitions\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is the maximum velocity in any of the components and peak vector sum (PVS) is the true vector sum of the three components.\n\nBlast vibration is made up of three different wave components; the transverse, vertical and longitudinal waves.\n\n## Vector Sum vs. Component PPV\n\nThe fundamental difference lies in how these metrics are calculated:\n\n- **Component PPV**: The maximum velocity in any of the components\n- **Vector Sum (PVS/Resultant)**: The amplitude of the vector sum of three time-synchronised velocity components directly measured by an instrument, calculated for every point in the waveform and finding the maximum value.\n\nThe maximum of the sums is the peak vector sum and does not necessarily occur at the PPV of an individual waveform.\n\n## Preference and Advantages\n\nThe peak vector sum (PVS) is often preferred over the PPV because it reflects the effect of the other two components. The terms Peak Vector Sum (PVS) and Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) are used interchangeably (although this is not always the case globally).\n\nThe distinction is important for blast assessment and regulation, as the PVS/PPV generally indicates the combined strength of all three waves when at their strongest.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"5cc9237e-26f9-486e-a241-77a9b8b84847","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:45.228Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration \"vector sum\" \"resultant\" vs component PPV peak particle velocity comparison","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01VwrykRfvSkRWxkaSEAWdKS","content":[{"title":"Final Report Role of blast design parameters on ground ...","url":"https://files.dep.state.pa.us/Mining/BureauOfMiningPrograms/BMPPortalFiles/Blasting_Research_Papers/2005%20Adhikari%20Role%20of%20Blast%20Design.pdf"},{"title":"Interpreting a blast report Printed: January 4, 2021 (V 10.72 - 10.72.1)","url":"https://ballaratgoldmine.com.au/wp-content/uploads/Interpreting-a-Blast-Report.pdf"},{"title":"Resultant Peak Particle Velocity against Scaled Distance for the Charge... | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Resultant-Peak-Particle-Velocity-against-Scaled-Distance-for-the-Charge-Sizes_fig1_274008170"},{"title":"(PDF) Relationship between peak particle acceleration, velocity and displacement of blast vibration","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/392403274_Relationship_between_peak_particle_acceleration_velocity_and_displacement_of_blast_vibration"},{"title":"Vibration and air pressure monitoring of seismic sources","url":"https://www.crewes.org/Documents/ResearchReports/2007/2007-16.pdf"},{"title":"Vector Sum and PPV - Texcel","url":"https://texcel.com.au/vector-sum-and-ppv/"},{"title":"Analysis of the Impact of ground vibration on the blast zone within","url":"https://www.bio-conferences.org/articles/bioconf/pdf/2024/65/bioconf_btmic2024_01067.pdf"},{"title":"Estimation of near-field peak particle velocity","url":"https://academicjournals.org/article/article1379508799_Arora%20and%20Dey.pdf"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"},{"title":"Peak particle velocity definition (From Instantel, 2001). | Download Scientific Diagram","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Peak-particle-velocity-definition-From-Instantel-2001_fig1_265347764"}]},"Based on the search results, here's a comparison of vector sum (resultant) versus component approaches for blast vibration PPV measurement:\n\n## Key Definitions\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is the maximum velocity in any of the components and peak vector sum (PVS) is the true vector sum of the three components.\n\nBlast vibration is made up of three different wave components; the transverse, vertical and longitudinal waves.\n\n## Vector Sum vs. Component PPV\n\nThe fundamental difference lies in how these metrics are calculated:\n\n- **Component PPV**: The maximum velocity in any of the components\n- **Vector Sum (PVS/Resultant)**: The amplitude of the vector sum of three time-synchronised velocity components directly measured by an instrument, calculated for every point in the waveform and finding the maximum value.\n\nThe maximum of the sums is the peak vector sum and does not necessarily occur at the PPV of an individual waveform.\n\n## Preference and Advantages\n\nThe peak vector sum (PVS) is often preferred over the PPV because it reflects the effect of the other two components. The terms Peak Vector Sum (PVS) and Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) are used interchangeably (although this is not always the case globally).\n\nThe distinction is important for blast assessment and regulation, as the PVS/PPV generally indicates the combined strength of all three waves when at their strongest."],"durationSeconds":6.698347833,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018vjBgZMRdUg1coA3t6cYvt","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration PPA peak particle acceleration attenuation prediction model empirical\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of Ground Vibration Velocity Induced by Long Hole Blasting Using a Particle Swarm Optimization Algorithm\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/14/9/3839\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Prediction of Peak Particle Velocity of Blast-induced Ground Vibrations using Boosted Regression Trees\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/362065291_Prediction_of_Peak_Particle_Velocity_of_Blast-induced_Ground_Vibrations_using_Boosted_Regression_Trees\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-81218-z\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Evaluation and prediction of blast-induced peak particle velocity using artificial neural network: A case study\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/324274892_Evaluation_and_prediction_of_blast-induced_peak_particle_velocity_using_artificial_neural_network_A_case_study\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search for blast vibration prediction models. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Main Prediction Methods\n\nThe most common methods for PPV (peak particle velocity) prediction include empirical equations, machine learning, and numerical simulation methods.\n\n## Empirical Models\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, and other factors are also considered in various empirical equations developed by various researchers. The Sadowsky empirical formula is commonly utilized for predicting the peak vibration velocity of particles.\n\n## Key Parameters\n\nThe peak particle velocity (PPV) is primarily influenced by factors including the maximum charge per delay (Q), the distance from the blasting working face to the test point (R), the scaled distance (SD), and the site attenuation coefficients (k and α).\n\n## Advanced Approaches\n\nRecent research has identified various machine learning models as effective alternatives, with PSO-SVR, GA-SVR-RBF, and FFA-SVR models providing more precise estimations of blasting vibration intensity.\n\nThe search results provide comprehensive information about empirical and predictive models used to assess blast vibration attenuation, particularly focusing on peak particle velocity (PPV) as the primary assessment metric.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"038ba551-fdef-40fd-8a46-9d5d9e973df8","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:47.153Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration PPA peak particle acceleration attenuation prediction model empirical","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01PqBcYciyAQTpHoX9k76oVp","content":[{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"Prediction of Ground Vibration Velocity Induced by Long Hole Blasting Using a Particle Swarm Optimization Algorithm","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3417/14/9/3839"},{"title":"(PDF) Prediction of Peak Particle Velocity of Blast-induced Ground Vibrations using Boosted Regression Trees","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/362065291_Prediction_of_Peak_Particle_Velocity_of_Blast-induced_Ground_Vibrations_using_Boosted_Regression_Trees"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-81218-z"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"},{"title":"(PDF) Evaluation and prediction of blast-induced peak particle velocity using artificial neural network: A case study","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/324274892_Evaluation_and_prediction_of_blast-induced_peak_particle_velocity_using_artificial_neural_network_A_case_study"}]},"I've performed the web search for blast vibration prediction models. Here's a summary of the key findings:\n\n## Main Prediction Methods\n\nThe most common methods for PPV (peak particle velocity) prediction include empirical equations, machine learning, and numerical simulation methods.\n\n## Empirical Models\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, and other factors are also considered in various empirical equations developed by various researchers. The Sadowsky empirical formula is commonly utilized for predicting the peak vibration velocity of particles.\n\n## Key Parameters\n\nThe peak particle velocity (PPV) is primarily influenced by factors including the maximum charge per delay (Q), the distance from the blasting working face to the test point (R), the scaled distance (SD), and the site attenuation coefficients (k and α).\n\n## Advanced Approaches\n\nRecent research has identified various machine learning models as effective alternatives, with PSO-SVR, GA-SVR-RBF, and FFA-SVR models providing more precise estimations of blasting vibration intensity.\n\nThe search results provide comprehensive information about empirical and predictive models used to assess blast vibration attenuation, particularly focusing on peak particle velocity (PPV) as the primary assessment metric."],"durationSeconds":6.968251041999996,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y8KLqP3E894CzPpy8gVAUb","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration physics-based source model numerical simulation limitations attenuation prediction\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Numerical Simulation and Field Monitoring of Blasting Vibration for Tunnel In-Situ Expansion by a Non-Cut Blast Scheme - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11280789/\"},{\"title\":\"Modelling the Source of Blasting for the Numerical Simulation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Review | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/308665913_Modelling_the_Source_of_Blasting_for_the_Numerical_Simulation_of_Blast-Induced_Ground_Vibrations_A_Review\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Numerical modelling of blast-induced ground vibrations\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326601233_Numerical_modelling_of_blast-induced_ground_vibrations\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Prediction of Blast Induced Vibration using Numerical Simulation\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318744047_Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Vibration_using_Numerical_Simulation\"},{\"title\":\"Knowledge mapping of research progress in blast-induced ground vibration from 1990 to 2022 using CiteSpace-based scientometric analysis | Environmental Science and Pollution Research | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11356-023-29712-1\"},{\"title\":\"Modelling the Source of Blasting for the Numerical Simulation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Review | Rock Mechanics and Rock Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00603-016-1101-2\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547\"}]\n\nI found relevant research on blast vibration physics-based modeling and numerical simulation. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Source Modeling Approaches\n\nSince detonation and rock fragmentation are highly complex phenomena, the equivalent cavity theory is applied in order to maintain a single elastic ground model. The instantaneous blasting process can be generally simplified as a triangular impulse load.\n\n## Numerical Simulation Methods\n\nIn recent decades, numerical simulation methods such as finite element method (FEM) and discrete element method (DEM) have been extensively applied to investigate blasting-induced vibrations. Numerical simulations are employed to analyze the dynamic response of the surrounding rock and the attenuation of vibrations as blasting excavation progresses near the fault-controlled zone.\n\n## Modeling Limitations\n\nIn dynamic problems, the fixed and elastic boundary may cause the \"unreal\" reflection of vibration wave; however, the FLAC3D platform provides a quiet boundary to absorb vibration energy and prevent \"unreal\" reflection, which can reasonably simulate the infinite rock mass.\n\nThe inherent nonlinear characteristics of blasting-induced vibrations require a mathematical framework capable of capturing the complex dynamic interactions between explosive loads and heterogeneous, nonlinear rock masses.\n\n## Validation and Prediction\n\nAn accurate model of the source of blast-induced vibrations is required to accurately predict ground vibration propagation. By employing a regression analysis, quantitative relationships can be established between the amount of explosive used and the peak particle velocity (PPV) at different distances to determine safe thresholds.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"f83e136d-8aae-41cf-9e17-4f1fb9032b07","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:48.174Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration physics-based source model numerical simulation limitations attenuation prediction","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_013zNT8ENrhuf5vYHvBJGN8H","content":[{"title":"Numerical Simulation and Field Monitoring of Blasting Vibration for Tunnel In-Situ Expansion by a Non-Cut Blast Scheme - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11280789/"},{"title":"Modelling the Source of Blasting for the Numerical Simulation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Review | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/308665913_Modelling_the_Source_of_Blasting_for_the_Numerical_Simulation_of_Blast-Induced_Ground_Vibrations_A_Review"},{"title":"(PDF) Numerical modelling of blast-induced ground vibrations","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/326601233_Numerical_modelling_of_blast-induced_ground_vibrations"},{"title":"(PDF) Prediction of Blast Induced Vibration using Numerical Simulation","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318744047_Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Vibration_using_Numerical_Simulation"},{"title":"Knowledge mapping of research progress in blast-induced ground vibration from 1990 to 2022 using CiteSpace-based scientometric analysis | Environmental Science and Pollution Research | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s11356-023-29712-1"},{"title":"Modelling the Source of Blasting for the Numerical Simulation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Review | Rock Mechanics and Rock Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00603-016-1101-2"},{"title":"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547"}]},"I found relevant research on blast vibration physics-based modeling and numerical simulation. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Source Modeling Approaches\n\nSince detonation and rock fragmentation are highly complex phenomena, the equivalent cavity theory is applied in order to maintain a single elastic ground model. The instantaneous blasting process can be generally simplified as a triangular impulse load.\n\n## Numerical Simulation Methods\n\nIn recent decades, numerical simulation methods such as finite element method (FEM) and discrete element method (DEM) have been extensively applied to investigate blasting-induced vibrations. Numerical simulations are employed to analyze the dynamic response of the surrounding rock and the attenuation of vibrations as blasting excavation progresses near the fault-controlled zone.\n\n## Modeling Limitations\n\nIn dynamic problems, the fixed and elastic boundary may cause the \"unreal\" reflection of vibration wave; however, the FLAC3D platform provides a quiet boundary to absorb vibration energy and prevent \"unreal\" reflection, which can reasonably simulate the infinite rock mass.\n\nThe inherent nonlinear characteristics of blasting-induced vibrations require a mathematical framework capable of capturing the complex dynamic interactions between explosive loads and heterogeneous, nonlinear rock masses.\n\n## Validation and Prediction\n\nAn accurate model of the source of blast-induced vibrations is required to accurately predict ground vibration propagation. By employing a regression analysis, quantitative relationships can be established between the amount of explosive used and the peak particle velocity (PPV) at different distances to determine safe thresholds."],"durationSeconds":8.070473333999995,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01E4GhwFRhgWNvBAKqNvqUQY","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration \"signature hole\" waveform scaling charge weight predicted PPV\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Charge Weight Scaling Laws and the Superposition of Blast Vibration Waves\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232922308_Charge_Weight_Scaling_Laws_and_the_Superposition_of_Blast_Vibration_Waves\"},{\"title\":\"Experimental and theoretical analysis of charge length on single-hole vibration amplitude from underground deep-hole blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160924002417\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Explosive charge mass and peak particle velocity (PPV)-frequency relation in mining blast\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231037548_Explosive_charge_mass_and_peak_particle_velocity_PPV-frequency_relation_in_mining_blast\"},{\"title\":\"Signature Hole Blast Vibration Control – Twenty Years Hence and Beyond\",\"url\":\"https://miningandblasting.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/signature-hole-blast-vibration-control-e28093-twenty-years-hence-and-beyond.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Signature Hole Blast Vibration Control – Twenty Years Hence and Beyond\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/13318263/Signature_Hole_Blast_Vibration_Control_Twenty_Years_Hence_and_Beyond\"},{\"title\":\"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/259522141_Blast_vibration_dependence_on_charge_length_velocity_of_detonation_and_layered_media\"},{\"title\":\"An integrated approach of signature hole vibration monitoring ...\",\"url\":\"https://miningandblasting.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/approach_of_signature_hole_vibration_monitoring_and_modeling_for_quarry_vibration_control.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547\"}]\n\nI found comprehensive research results on this topic. Here's what the web search revealed:\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nSignature hole waveforms are generated using electronic delay detonators, with PPV values controlled by maintaining delay intervals of 25 ms and 67 ms between holes and rows, respectively.\n\nThe single borehole waveform is fundamental and always acts as the signature for analyzing and predicting blast vibration characteristics.\n\n## Waveform Scaling and Prediction\n\nStandard charge weight scaling laws consider the distance and the maximum charge per delay interval as the only technological factors influencing peak particle velocity (PPV). However, practical experience indicates that drilling and blasting technology significantly contribute to the PPV.\n\nThe \"signature hole method\" applies linear superposition of single-hole blasting waveforms to predict multi-hole blasting vibrations. Blast vibration can be simulated by detonating a signature hole with vibration monitored at critical locations, and then using a computer to superpose the waveforms with varying delays to create destructive interference at frequencies favored by local geology.\n\n## Charge Weight Effects\n\nA greater length-to-diameter ratio with the same charge weight makes a smaller PPV. Additionally, the increasing charge length, coupled with rising charge weight, prompts closer examination of induced vibration and traditional vibration prediction laws.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"14def559-daeb-4377-bfe7-fe2cbf92ca62","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:22:50.149Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration \"signature hole\" waveform scaling charge weight predicted PPV","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01QH96qxjiwGDhw2nn66hogB","content":[{"title":"Charge Weight Scaling Laws and the Superposition of Blast Vibration Waves","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/232922308_Charge_Weight_Scaling_Laws_and_the_Superposition_of_Blast_Vibration_Waves"},{"title":"Experimental and theoretical analysis of charge length on single-hole vibration amplitude from underground deep-hole blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160924002417"},{"title":"(PDF) Explosive charge mass and peak particle velocity (PPV)-frequency relation in mining blast","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231037548_Explosive_charge_mass_and_peak_particle_velocity_PPV-frequency_relation_in_mining_blast"},{"title":"Signature Hole Blast Vibration Control – Twenty Years Hence and Beyond","url":"https://miningandblasting.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/signature-hole-blast-vibration-control-e28093-twenty-years-hence-and-beyond.pdf"},{"title":"(PDF) Signature Hole Blast Vibration Control – Twenty Years Hence and Beyond","url":"https://www.academia.edu/13318263/Signature_Hole_Blast_Vibration_Control_Twenty_Years_Hence_and_Beyond"},{"title":"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/259522141_Blast_vibration_dependence_on_charge_length_velocity_of_detonation_and_layered_media"},{"title":"An integrated approach of signature hole vibration monitoring ...","url":"https://miningandblasting.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/approach_of_signature_hole_vibration_monitoring_and_modeling_for_quarry_vibration_control.pdf"},{"title":"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547"}]},"I found comprehensive research results on this topic. Here's what the web search revealed:\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nSignature hole waveforms are generated using electronic delay detonators, with PPV values controlled by maintaining delay intervals of 25 ms and 67 ms between holes and rows, respectively.\n\nThe single borehole waveform is fundamental and always acts as the signature for analyzing and predicting blast vibration characteristics.\n\n## Waveform Scaling and Prediction\n\nStandard charge weight scaling laws consider the distance and the maximum charge per delay interval as the only technological factors influencing peak particle velocity (PPV). However, practical experience indicates that drilling and blasting technology significantly contribute to the PPV.\n\nThe \"signature hole method\" applies linear superposition of single-hole blasting waveforms to predict multi-hole blasting vibrations. Blast vibration can be simulated by detonating a signature hole with vibration monitored at critical locations, and then using a computer to superpose the waveforms with varying delays to create destructive interference at frequencies favored by local geology.\n\n## Charge Weight Effects\n\nA greater length-to-diameter ratio with the same charge weight makes a smaller PPV. Additionally, the increasing charge length, coupled with rising charge weight, prompts closer examination of induced vibration and traditional vibration prediction laws."],"durationSeconds":10.660903125000004,"searchCount":1}}
