{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MxS2QFPvGiFwBhKoFFUwG5","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration \"three components\" \"longitudinal transverse vertical\" separately modelled attenuation equation\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X\"},{\"title\":\"Propagation and attenuation characteristics of various ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726100000026\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8548544/\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Prediction of Blast Induced Vibration using Numerical Simulation\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318744047_Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Vibration_using_Numerical_Simulation\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about blast vibration monitoring with three directional components:\n\nPropagation characteristics of vibrations generated by various sources may be dependent on the type of the generated waves, which can be assessed by measuring particle motions in three directions including vertical, longitudinal, and transverse directions.\n\nLongitudinal, transversal and the vertical ground motion component are analyzed at three monitoring points that are located at different distances from the blasting source.\n\nRegarding attenuation equations, the search results show that V = k(p₀/ρcₚ)(b/R)^α, where k is the improvement factor associated with the blasting conditions, p₀ the initial pressure of the blast-generated gas in the hole, b the radius of the hole, ρ the rock density, R the distance of the detonation point from the particle, cₚ the rock longitudinal wave velocity, and α the blast vibration attenuation index.\n\nHowever, the search results do not specifically address whether the three components (longitudinal, transverse, and vertical) are separately modelled with individual attenuation equations. The results show these components are measured and analyzed separately, but do not provide evidence of different attenuation equations for each directional component.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"ce598bfb-ef23-4e6d-8a6b-f6689c4d5367","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:23:03.808Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration \"three components\" \"longitudinal transverse vertical\" separately modelled attenuation equation","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01BoXfyMGeSuVhvMnMJ92vr2","content":[{"title":"Determination of the ground vibration attenuation law from a single blast: A particular case of trench blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552100086X"},{"title":"Propagation and attenuation characteristics of various ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726100000026"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8548544/"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/"},{"title":"(PDF) Prediction of Blast Induced Vibration using Numerical Simulation","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318744047_Prediction_of_Blast_Induced_Vibration_using_Numerical_Simulation"}]},"Based on the search results, I found relevant information about blast vibration monitoring with three directional components:\n\nPropagation characteristics of vibrations generated by various sources may be dependent on the type of the generated waves, which can be assessed by measuring particle motions in three directions including vertical, longitudinal, and transverse directions.\n\nLongitudinal, transversal and the vertical ground motion component are analyzed at three monitoring points that are located at different distances from the blasting source.\n\nRegarding attenuation equations, the search results show that V = k(p₀/ρcₚ)(b/R)^α, where k is the improvement factor associated with the blasting conditions, p₀ the initial pressure of the blast-generated gas in the hole, b the radius of the hole, ρ the rock density, R the distance of the detonation point from the particle, cₚ the rock longitudinal wave velocity, and α the blast vibration attenuation index.\n\nHowever, the search results do not specifically address whether the three components (longitudinal, transverse, and vertical) are separately modelled with individual attenuation equations. The results show these components are measured and analyzed separately, but do not provide evidence of different attenuation equations for each directional component."],"durationSeconds":5.946473500000008,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ffu8RDiMaSsDFTNPhEEzKy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Blair 2004 2009 blast vibration simulation stochastic prediction model components\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547\"},{\"title\":\"Stochastic Modeling Approach for the Evaluation of Backbreak due to Blasting Operations in Open Pit Mines | Rock Mechanics and Rock Engineering | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00603-013-0438-z\"},{\"title\":\"Research on non-stationary blasting vibration prediction models and analysis of equal-interval delay for vibration reduction | Bulletin of Engineering Geology and the Environment\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10064-025-04718-w\"},{\"title\":\"Development and Application of Blasting Vibration Prediction Model | springerprofessional.de\",\"url\":\"https://www.springerprofessional.de/en/development-and-application-of-blasting-vibration-prediction-mod/50777688\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Development of a blast-induced vibration prediction model using an artificial neural network\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/331398409_Development_of_a_blast-induced_vibration_prediction_model_using_an_artificial_neural_network\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"},{\"title\":\"Review of predicting the blast-induced ground vibrations to reduce impacts on ambient urban communities - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0959652620311823\"},{\"title\":\"Predicting blast-induced ground vibration using various types of neural networks - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726110001193\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I found relevant information about Blair's work on blast vibration simulation and prediction models:\n\nBlair introduced weighted scaled distance and nonlinear superposition models for blast vibration prediction. Blair depicted how the separate influence of variables, like weight and type of explosive used per delay, the delay time sequence, scatter in that sequence, spatial pattern of blastholes and properties of the transmitting medium, may be analyzed using a Monte Carlo model that has a predictive power superior to that of the traditional charge weight scaling laws.\n\nAdditionally, Blair and Armstrong pointed out that ground vibrations are affected by the condition of rock mass surrounding the blast hole and the vibrations were found to be independent of burden.\n\nThe search results show that Blair's work contributed significantly to the development of blast vibration prediction methodologies, particularly through stochastic modeling approaches using Monte Carlo simulation that account for multiple variables and randomness in blasting parameters, rather than relying solely on traditional empirical formulas based on charge weight scaling.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"6e03ede4-dc7f-4010-8f51-de0a445376e1","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:23:05.739Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Blair 2004 2009 blast vibration simulation stochastic prediction model components","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GHs9gSeFHKVXtVRbZZNUa7","content":[{"title":"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547"},{"title":"Stochastic Modeling Approach for the Evaluation of Backbreak due to Blasting Operations in Open Pit Mines | Rock Mechanics and Rock Engineering | Springer Nature Link","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00603-013-0438-z"},{"title":"Research on non-stationary blasting vibration prediction models and analysis of equal-interval delay for vibration reduction | Bulletin of Engineering Geology and the Environment","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s10064-025-04718-w"},{"title":"Development and Application of Blasting Vibration Prediction Model | springerprofessional.de","url":"https://www.springerprofessional.de/en/development-and-application-of-blasting-vibration-prediction-mod/50777688"},{"title":"(PDF) Development of a blast-induced vibration prediction model using an artificial neural network","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/331398409_Development_of_a_blast-induced_vibration_prediction_model_using_an_artificial_neural_network"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"},{"title":"Review of predicting the blast-induced ground vibrations to reduce impacts on ambient urban communities - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0959652620311823"},{"title":"Predicting blast-induced ground vibration using various types of neural networks - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726110001193"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/"}]},"Based on the web search results, I found relevant information about Blair's work on blast vibration simulation and prediction models:\n\nBlair introduced weighted scaled distance and nonlinear superposition models for blast vibration prediction. Blair depicted how the separate influence of variables, like weight and type of explosive used per delay, the delay time sequence, scatter in that sequence, spatial pattern of blastholes and properties of the transmitting medium, may be analyzed using a Monte Carlo model that has a predictive power superior to that of the traditional charge weight scaling laws.\n\nAdditionally, Blair and Armstrong pointed out that ground vibrations are affected by the condition of rock mass surrounding the blast hole and the vibrations were found to be independent of burden.\n\nThe search results show that Blair's work contributed significantly to the development of blast vibration prediction methodologies, particularly through stochastic modeling approaches using Monte Carlo simulation that account for multiple variables and randomness in blasting parameters, rather than relying solely on traditional empirical formulas based on charge weight scaling."],"durationSeconds":5.774099833999993,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S6Ad58BfhUw8wcJZ7qV97y","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Dowding blast vibration structure damage PPV component measurement monitoring\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Blast and Construction Vibration Monitoring and Control: Thirty-Five-Year Perspective\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245492635_Blast_and_Construction_Vibration_Monitoring_and_Control_Thirty-Five-Year_Perspective\"},{\"title\":\"Blasting Vibration Monitoring and a New Vibration Reduction Measure | Journal of Engineering and Technological Sciences\",\"url\":\"https://journals.itb.ac.id/index.php/jets/article/view/14886\"},{\"title\":\"Drawbacks of Blast Vibration Regulations\",\"url\":\"https://vulcanhammer.net/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/blst-crt.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Cumulative damage characteristics of tunnel initial support concrete under blasting load\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11757710/\"},{\"title\":\"Suggested method for blast vibration monitoring - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/014890629292124U\"},{\"title\":\"Application of strain gauge and geophone based integrated monitoring system in the measurements of the blast induced low-frequency vibrations | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-94128-5\"},{\"title\":\"Vibration analysis for blasting\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9927340\"},{\"title\":\"SOP FOR CONTROLLED BLASTING Vibration monitoring ...\",\"url\":\"https://www.cmpdi.co.in/sites/default/files/2022-11/SOP%20FOR%20CONTROLLED%20BLASTING.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration, structure damage, PPV component measurement, and monitoring:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and Structural Damage:**\nStructural damage can be well correlated with the peak particle velocity (PPV) of ground vibrations. The criterion for structural damage of residential buildings was set at 51 mm/s (2 in/s) peak particle velocity in the frequency range of 3-100 Hz.\n\n**Dowding's Contributions:**\nDowding (1996) developed approaches to control vibrations, and Dowding's special control lines employ several less restrictive bounds for close-in urban blasting.\n\n**Measurement Components:**\nVibrations in structures are analyzed based on measured motions of particles, determining peaks in velocity, acceleration or displacement, referred to as PPV (peak particle velocity), PPA (peak particle acceleration), or PPD (peak particle displacement).\n\n**PPV Monitoring:**\nPPV measurements can be conducted at three different directions, and FFT reports provide the full waveform of vibration resulting from blasting.\n\n**Structure Response to Frequency:**\nPPV on walls increases with their height, and PPV and principal frequency of different structures of single-story brick-concrete buildings are different.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"86da6675-669c-4104-bc92-9adcf66b204b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:23:06.412Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Dowding blast vibration structure damage PPV component measurement monitoring","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01Mij48hMRX8nLnedCiCnDGP","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Blast and Construction Vibration Monitoring and Control: Thirty-Five-Year Perspective","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/245492635_Blast_and_Construction_Vibration_Monitoring_and_Control_Thirty-Five-Year_Perspective"},{"title":"Blasting Vibration Monitoring and a New Vibration Reduction Measure | Journal of Engineering and Technological Sciences","url":"https://journals.itb.ac.id/index.php/jets/article/view/14886"},{"title":"Drawbacks of Blast Vibration Regulations","url":"https://vulcanhammer.net/wp-content/uploads/2017/01/blst-crt.pdf"},{"title":"Cumulative damage characteristics of tunnel initial support concrete under blasting load","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11757710/"},{"title":"Suggested method for blast vibration monitoring - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/014890629292124U"},{"title":"Application of strain gauge and geophone based integrated monitoring system in the measurements of the blast induced low-frequency vibrations | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-94128-5"},{"title":"Vibration analysis for blasting","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/9927340"},{"title":"SOP FOR CONTROLLED BLASTING Vibration monitoring ...","url":"https://www.cmpdi.co.in/sites/default/files/2022-11/SOP%20FOR%20CONTROLLED%20BLASTING.pdf"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration, structure damage, PPV component measurement, and monitoring:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and Structural Damage:**\nStructural damage can be well correlated with the peak particle velocity (PPV) of ground vibrations. The criterion for structural damage of residential buildings was set at 51 mm/s (2 in/s) peak particle velocity in the frequency range of 3-100 Hz.\n\n**Dowding's Contributions:**\nDowding (1996) developed approaches to control vibrations, and Dowding's special control lines employ several less restrictive bounds for close-in urban blasting.\n\n**Measurement Components:**\nVibrations in structures are analyzed based on measured motions of particles, determining peaks in velocity, acceleration or displacement, referred to as PPV (peak particle velocity), PPA (peak particle acceleration), or PPD (peak particle displacement).\n\n**PPV Monitoring:**\nPPV measurements can be conducted at three different directions, and FFT reports provide the full waveform of vibration resulting from blasting.\n\n**Structure Response to Frequency:**\nPPV on walls increases with their height, and PPV and principal frequency of different structures of single-story brick-concrete buildings are different."],"durationSeconds":7.120804082999995,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01S5xEujKzzZkTeFpuAza9LT","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration waveform simulation time history synthesis convolve superposition component\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Coupling of two methods, waveform superposition and numerical, to model blast vibration effect on slope stability in jointed rock masses - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0266352X14000792\"},{\"title\":\"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | A Method for Multihole Blasting Seismic Wave Prediction and Its Application in Pillar Recovery\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/physics/articles/10.3389/fphy.2021.569453/full\"},{\"title\":\"Modelling of blast vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0148906288909849\"},{\"title\":\"Full Waveform Prediction of Blasting Vibration Using Deep Learning\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/14/13/8200\"},{\"title\":\"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547\"},{\"title\":\"Vibration Control of Stress Waveform Superposition in ...\",\"url\":\"https://iieta.org/sites/default/files/Journals/MMC/MMC_B/86.2_18.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023\"},{\"title\":\"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about blast vibration waveform simulation using time history synthesis with convolution and superposition:\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nSeveral methods exist for predicting ground vibrations caused by mine blasting operations: empirical, artificial intelligence, waveform superposition, and numerical.\n\n## Convolution and Single-Shot Signal\n\nA fundamental technique involves assuming linear superposition of seismic effects of individual holes in a row shot, where the single shot signal is convolved with an impulse series that represents the firing sequence.\n\n## Waveform Superposition Method\n\nIf lithology near the blasting location is unchanged, measured single-hole vibration waveform from a test can be superimposed upon to obtain peak vibration velocity at each observation position and predict the vibration intensity of production blast. In the calculation process, each wavelet is superimposed in the order of delay and the distance from the measurement point.\n\n## Time History Synthesis\n\nModeled time history of the blast is then used as a boundary condition to numerically simulate the vibration at any given point within the rock mass. Additionally, by introducing the variation of magnitude spectrum and phase spectrum with propagation distance and random parameters within confidence intervals, time-domain reconstruction of blasting vibration waveform can be realized.\n\n## Limitations\n\nThe assumption of linear time-invariant systems inherently limits prediction accuracy when applied to the nonlinear characteristics exhibited by blasting vibrations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"d681fa9c-c4bd-4e72-b6be-02ea3a59b7a9","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:23:10.434Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration waveform simulation time history synthesis convolve superposition component","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01KeWdtarHhxC6MiodwUZvWa","content":[{"title":"Coupling of two methods, waveform superposition and numerical, to model blast vibration effect on slope stability in jointed rock masses - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0266352X14000792"},{"title":"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017"},{"title":"Frontiers | A Method for Multihole Blasting Seismic Wave Prediction and Its Application in Pillar Recovery","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/physics/articles/10.3389/fphy.2021.569453/full"},{"title":"Modelling of blast vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/0148906288909849"},{"title":"Full Waveform Prediction of Blasting Vibration Using Deep Learning","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2071-1050/14/13/8200"},{"title":"Nonlinear identification and waveform prediction of blast-induced ground vibrations using regularized Volterra series - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0888327026003547"},{"title":"Vibration Control of Stress Waveform Superposition in ...","url":"https://iieta.org/sites/default/files/Journals/MMC/MMC_B/86.2_18.pdf"},{"title":"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023"},{"title":"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found about blast vibration waveform simulation using time history synthesis with convolution and superposition:\n\n## Key Concepts\n\nSeveral methods exist for predicting ground vibrations caused by mine blasting operations: empirical, artificial intelligence, waveform superposition, and numerical.\n\n## Convolution and Single-Shot Signal\n\nA fundamental technique involves assuming linear superposition of seismic effects of individual holes in a row shot, where the single shot signal is convolved with an impulse series that represents the firing sequence.\n\n## Waveform Superposition Method\n\nIf lithology near the blasting location is unchanged, measured single-hole vibration waveform from a test can be superimposed upon to obtain peak vibration velocity at each observation position and predict the vibration intensity of production blast. In the calculation process, each wavelet is superimposed in the order of delay and the distance from the measurement point.\n\n## Time History Synthesis\n\nModeled time history of the blast is then used as a boundary condition to numerically simulate the vibration at any given point within the rock mass. Additionally, by introducing the variation of magnitude spectrum and phase spectrum with propagation distance and random parameters within confidence intervals, time-domain reconstruction of blasting vibration waveform can be realized.\n\n## Limitations\n\nThe assumption of linear time-invariant systems inherently limits prediction accuracy when applied to the nonlinear characteristics exhibited by blasting vibrations."],"durationSeconds":11.621193250000012,"searchCount":1}}
