{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WNWCYURtAKvSp9afLFq85q","type":"tool_result","content":"# Blast Vibration Component Modeling: Key Findings\n\n## Component Treatment\n\nThe research analyzes blast-induced vibrations by examining three motion components separately:\n\n- **Longitudinal (L)**: Motion parallel to blast direction\n- **Transversal (T)**: Lateral motion perpendicular to blast direction  \n- **Vertical (V)**: Up-and-down ground motion\n\nThe authors state: \"Longitudinal, transversal and the vertical ground motion component are analyzed at three monitoring points that are located at different distances from the blasting source.\"\n\n## Separate vs. Joint Analysis\n\n**For stochasticity testing:** Components are analyzed independently. The surrogate data analysis and determinism tests examine each directional component separately at each measuring station, treating them as distinct time series.\n\n**For PPV prediction:** The model combines parameters into a single neural network but doesn't explicitly model component correlations. The four input parameters—total charge, maximum charge per delay, distance, and hole depth—predict overall peak particle velocity without considering directional coupling effects.\n\n## Stochastic Prediction Methods\n\nThe authors establish that recorded vibrations belong to \"a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs, which could be distorted by a monotonic, instantaneous, time-independent nonlinear function.\" Their artificial neural network achieves R² = 0.94, substantially outperforming conventional empirical predictors (R² = 0.54–0.66).\n\n## Multivariate Response Consideration\n\nThe study does not explicitly model component interdependencies or correlated responses across directional axes."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"e8071e2a-dd7a-4a2f-822c-63658e02e9f4","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:23:26.947Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":196830,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Blast Vibration Component Modeling: Key Findings\n\n## Component Treatment\n\nThe research analyzes blast-induced vibrations by examining three motion components separately:\n\n- **Longitudinal (L)**: Motion parallel to blast direction\n- **Transversal (T)**: Lateral motion perpendicular to blast direction  \n- **Vertical (V)**: Up-and-down ground motion\n\nThe authors state: \"Longitudinal, transversal and the vertical ground motion component are analyzed at three monitoring points that are located at different distances from the blasting source.\"\n\n## Separate vs. Joint Analysis\n\n**For stochasticity testing:** Components are analyzed independently. The surrogate data analysis and determinism tests examine each directional component separately at each measuring station, treating them as distinct time series.\n\n**For PPV prediction:** The model combines parameters into a single neural network but doesn't explicitly model component correlations. The four input parameters—total charge, maximum charge per delay, distance, and hole depth—predict overall peak particle velocity without considering directional coupling effects.\n\n## Stochastic Prediction Methods\n\nThe authors establish that recorded vibrations belong to \"a class of stationary linear stochastic processes with Gaussian inputs, which could be distorted by a monotonic, instantaneous, time-independent nonlinear function.\" Their artificial neural network achieves R² = 0.94, substantially outperforming conventional empirical predictors (R² = 0.54–0.66).\n\n## Multivariate Response Consideration\n\nThe study does not explicitly model component interdependencies or correlated responses across directional axes.","durationMs":7625,"url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3866117/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Au2yQukBK2qrFZgfr8zMi9","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration \"peak particle acceleration\" PPA \"peak particle velocity\" PPV frequency attenuation empirical model separate\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Explosive charge mass and peak particle velocity (PPV)-frequency relation in mining blast\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231037548_Explosive_charge_mass_and_peak_particle_velocity_PPV-frequency_relation_in_mining_blast\"},{\"title\":\"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"},{\"title\":\"Determination of blast-induced ground vibration equations for rocks using mechanical and geological properties - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477551600024X\"},{\"title\":\"Explosive charge mass and peak particle velocity (PPV)-frequency relation in mining blast | Journal of Geophysics and Engineering | Oxford Academic\",\"url\":\"https://academic.oup.com/jge/article/7/3/223/5127516\"},{\"title\":\"Blast Vibration Criteria Selection\",\"url\":\"https://www.eweb.org/documents/Projects/Water%20Storage/Blast-Vibration-Criteria-Selection.pdf\"}]\n\nI've completed the web search for your query about blast vibration, peak particle acceleration (PPA), peak particle velocity (PPV), frequency, attenuation, and empirical models. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Empirical Models for PPV and Blast Vibration Attenuation\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, with various empirical equations developed by researchers. When blast vibrations cannot be measured with seismographs, empirical formulas are commonly used to predict vibration by specifying the PPV-scale distance relationship.\n\n## Peak Particle Velocity and Acceleration\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is one of main indicators for determining the extent of ground vibration. Research has also focused on regression analyses of peak particle acceleration (PPA).\n\n## Frequency-Related Approaches\n\nResearchers have proposed formulas that relate PPV prediction in mine blasting with the frequency of generated seismic waves and developed new prediction equations that include frequency and blasting relationship. Additionally, the Ricker formula (1977) is widely used in geophysical engineering and seismology to model the absorptive properties (attenuation) of the Earth.\n\n## Factors Affecting Attenuation\n\nVibration attenuates fastest if it propagates perpendicular to rock joint sets, and the presence of water table and soil-rock interface affects the slope of attenuation curve.\n\nThe search results indicate that modern research is increasingly incorporating machine learning approaches alongside traditional empirical models for improved prediction accuracy.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"201cb38f-0182-4dc3-ab77-68810b22564f","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:23:27.569Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration \"peak particle acceleration\" PPA \"peak particle velocity\" PPV frequency attenuation empirical model separate","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_012N822fof7949PYg6pG4RsT","content":[{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"(PDF) Explosive charge mass and peak particle velocity (PPV)-frequency relation in mining blast","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231037548_Explosive_charge_mass_and_peak_particle_velocity_PPV-frequency_relation_in_mining_blast"},{"title":"Assessment of peak particle velocity of blast vibration using hybrid soft computing approaches | Journal of Computational Design and Engineering | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jcde/article/12/2/154/7959003"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"},{"title":"Determination of blast-induced ground vibration equations for rocks using mechanical and geological properties - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477551600024X"},{"title":"Explosive charge mass and peak particle velocity (PPV)-frequency relation in mining blast | Journal of Geophysics and Engineering | Oxford Academic","url":"https://academic.oup.com/jge/article/7/3/223/5127516"},{"title":"Blast Vibration Criteria Selection","url":"https://www.eweb.org/documents/Projects/Water%20Storage/Blast-Vibration-Criteria-Selection.pdf"}]},"I've completed the web search for your query about blast vibration, peak particle acceleration (PPA), peak particle velocity (PPV), frequency, attenuation, and empirical models. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Empirical Models for PPV and Blast Vibration Attenuation\n\nThe widely adopted empirical model to predict PPV is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, with various empirical equations developed by researchers. When blast vibrations cannot be measured with seismographs, empirical formulas are commonly used to predict vibration by specifying the PPV-scale distance relationship.\n\n## Peak Particle Velocity and Acceleration\n\nPeak particle velocity (PPV) is one of main indicators for determining the extent of ground vibration. Research has also focused on regression analyses of peak particle acceleration (PPA).\n\n## Frequency-Related Approaches\n\nResearchers have proposed formulas that relate PPV prediction in mine blasting with the frequency of generated seismic waves and developed new prediction equations that include frequency and blasting relationship. Additionally, the Ricker formula (1977) is widely used in geophysical engineering and seismology to model the absorptive properties (attenuation) of the Earth.\n\n## Factors Affecting Attenuation\n\nVibration attenuates fastest if it propagates perpendicular to rock joint sets, and the presence of water table and soil-rock interface affects the slope of attenuation curve.\n\nThe search results indicate that modern research is increasingly incorporating machine learning approaches alongside traditional empirical models for improved prediction accuracy."],"durationSeconds":7.681202707999997,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018XrMCYk5zzSjiskuXrk5Hj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"response spectrum\" blast vibration \"input motion\" \"time history\" scaling method earthquake analogy\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Shock Response Spectrum (SRS)\",\"url\":\"https://community.sw.siemens.com/s/article/shock-response-spectrum-srs\"},{\"title\":\"In recent studies, spectral matching is the most commonly proposed method for selecting earthquake records for time-history analysis of structures. However, until now, there have been no serious investigations of the effects of coordinate values on the scaling of ground motions. This paper investigated the influence of using arithmetic and logarithmic values of response spectra in spectral matching procedures (i.e., ASM and LSM methods) on the results of nonlinear structural time-history analysis. Steel mom\",\"url\":\"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/sv/2020/8180612/\"},{\"title\":\"Practical Introduction to Shock Waveform and Shock Response Spectrum - MATLAB & Simulink\",\"url\":\"https://www.mathworks.com/help/signal/ug/practical-introduction-to-shock-waveform-and-shock-response-spectrum.html\"},{\"title\":\"ERDC/CHL CHETN-VI-41 December 2005 Determination of Standard Response\",\"url\":\"https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/pdfs/ADA441347.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of efficiency index of friction energy dissipation devices   using endurance time method\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.04530\"},{\"title\":\"Response Spectrum Analysis Definition\",\"url\":\"https://www.comsol.com/multiphysics/response-spectrum-analysis\"},{\"title\":\"Shock Response Spectrum (SRS): The Comprehensive Guide\",\"url\":\"https://caeflow.com/vibration_and_acoustics/shock-response-spectrum-srs/\"},{\"title\":\"Performance-based optimal distribution of viscous dampers in structure   using hysteretic energy compatible endurance time excitations\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.12983\"},{\"title\":\"Response Spectrum vs Random vibration | Ansys Learning Forum\",\"url\":\"https://innovationspace.ansys.com/forum/forums/topic/response-spectrum-vs-random-vibration/\"}]\n\nI've completed a web search on response spectra, blast vibration, and time-history scaling methods. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Response Spectrum Fundamentals:**\nA transient acceleration-time history input is applied to a base on which a series of linear oscillators of different natural frequencies are mounted. An acceleration response spectrum represents the peak accelerations that a suite of SDOF systems with a range of natural periods may exhibit when subject to a given ground motion component.\n\n**Time History Analysis and Scaling Methods:**\nSpectral matching methods that use arithmetic values and logarithmic values of response spectra are referred to as the ASM and LSM method, respectively. Matching the response spectra at the fundamental period of structure or considering period range (0.2T to 1.5T) can be conventionally adopted for scaling records to the desired earthquake hazard levels.\n\n**Time History Synthesis:**\nTime history synthesis involves generating waveforms matching target spectrum specifications. Common methods include summing damped sinusoids or using wavelet-based approaches. Additionally, multiple valid time histories can produce identical SRS curves due to the non-unique relationship between waveforms and spectra.\n\n**Shock Response Spectra (SRS):**\nA Shock Response Spectrum is a method to estimate the peak response of a system to the transient base motion input. Different damping ratios produce different SRS curves for identical shock waveforms, with zero damping producing maximum response while high damping flattens the spectrum.\n\nThese concepts apply across various transient events including blasts and earthquakes.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"95443271-c87e-49ad-b350-77798e45c3de","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:23:28.554Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"response spectrum\" blast vibration \"input motion\" \"time history\" scaling method earthquake analogy","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014MzSPdGuozv24WxU6D2W5P","content":[{"title":"Shock Response Spectrum (SRS)","url":"https://community.sw.siemens.com/s/article/shock-response-spectrum-srs"},{"title":"In recent studies, spectral matching is the most commonly proposed method for selecting earthquake records for time-history analysis of structures. However, until now, there have been no serious investigations of the effects of coordinate values on the scaling of ground motions. This paper investigated the influence of using arithmetic and logarithmic values of response spectra in spectral matching procedures (i.e., ASM and LSM methods) on the results of nonlinear structural time-history analysis. Steel mom","url":"https://www.hindawi.com/journals/sv/2020/8180612/"},{"title":"Practical Introduction to Shock Waveform and Shock Response Spectrum - MATLAB & Simulink","url":"https://www.mathworks.com/help/signal/ug/practical-introduction-to-shock-waveform-and-shock-response-spectrum.html"},{"title":"ERDC/CHL CHETN-VI-41 December 2005 Determination of Standard Response","url":"https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/pdfs/ADA441347.pdf"},{"title":"Evaluation of efficiency index of friction energy dissipation devices   using endurance time method","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.04530"},{"title":"Response Spectrum Analysis Definition","url":"https://www.comsol.com/multiphysics/response-spectrum-analysis"},{"title":"Shock Response Spectrum (SRS): The Comprehensive Guide","url":"https://caeflow.com/vibration_and_acoustics/shock-response-spectrum-srs/"},{"title":"Performance-based optimal distribution of viscous dampers in structure   using hysteretic energy compatible endurance time excitations","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2107.12983"},{"title":"Response Spectrum vs Random vibration | Ansys Learning Forum","url":"https://innovationspace.ansys.com/forum/forums/topic/response-spectrum-vs-random-vibration/"}]},"I've completed a web search on response spectra, blast vibration, and time-history scaling methods. Here's what I found:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Response Spectrum Fundamentals:**\nA transient acceleration-time history input is applied to a base on which a series of linear oscillators of different natural frequencies are mounted. An acceleration response spectrum represents the peak accelerations that a suite of SDOF systems with a range of natural periods may exhibit when subject to a given ground motion component.\n\n**Time History Analysis and Scaling Methods:**\nSpectral matching methods that use arithmetic values and logarithmic values of response spectra are referred to as the ASM and LSM method, respectively. Matching the response spectra at the fundamental period of structure or considering period range (0.2T to 1.5T) can be conventionally adopted for scaling records to the desired earthquake hazard levels.\n\n**Time History Synthesis:**\nTime history synthesis involves generating waveforms matching target spectrum specifications. Common methods include summing damped sinusoids or using wavelet-based approaches. Additionally, multiple valid time histories can produce identical SRS curves due to the non-unique relationship between waveforms and spectra.\n\n**Shock Response Spectra (SRS):**\nA Shock Response Spectrum is a method to estimate the peak response of a system to the transient base motion input. Different damping ratios produce different SRS curves for identical shock waveforms, with zero damping producing maximum response while high damping flattens the spectrum.\n\nThese concepts apply across various transient events including blasts and earthquakes."],"durationSeconds":7.957216667000001,"searchCount":1}}
