{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fuz7QisnJE8fcMjG5fnmTK","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration \"component attenuation\" prediction separate axes site-specific empirical model\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Expansion of Blast Vibration Attenuation Equations for Deeply Buried Small Clearance Tunnels Based on Dimensional Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.889504/full\"},{\"title\":\"Development of a blast-induced vibration prediction model using an artificial neural network\",\"url\":\"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532019000200013\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One\",\"url\":\"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"Accurate prediction of blast-induced ground vibration intensity using optimized machine learning models - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214914725002028\"},{\"title\":\"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/\"}]\n\nI found relevant research on blast vibration prediction models. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Empirical Models for Blast Vibration Prediction\n\nRecent research has established empirical prediction equations for blasting vibration propagation and attenuation, with separate equations for PPV (peak particle velocity, resultant velocity) and PPVi (maximum value of the three component velocities; i = x, y, z represent peak component particle velocity).\n\n## Site-Specific Parameters\n\nSite-specific constants k and β describe the characteristic of propagating media, with λ representing the inelastic attenuation factor of the site, and n representing the energy diminishing influenced by rock properties and geological discontinuities (e.g., faults, fractures, joints, fissures, and bedding planes). With the increase of the GSI (Geological Strength Index) of the surrounding rock mass, the site coefficients β and k of the blasting vibration prediction equation show a decreasing trend as a whole.\n\n## Component Attenuation Considerations\n\nA typical blasting vibration wave is a composite wave, and its attenuation law is affected by the type of dominant wave component. Researchers have established attenuation equations of peak particle velocity taking into account the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves in the blasting vibration wave.\n\n## Improving Model Accuracy\n\nBy using site-specific structural discontinuities as input, model performance can be further improved.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"779f22a5-d57a-428b-a708-81f47ad0ad79","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:24:55.714Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration \"component attenuation\" prediction separate axes site-specific empirical model","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_013B1nMWFTcToCZqmhzy5QzF","content":[{"title":"Frontiers | Empirical prediction of blast-induced vibration on adjacent tunnels","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/ecology-and-evolution/articles/10.3389/fevo.2023.1212654/full"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"},{"title":"Frontiers | Expansion of Blast Vibration Attenuation Equations for Deeply Buried Small Clearance Tunnels Based on Dimensional Analysis","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.889504/full"},{"title":"Development of a blast-induced vibration prediction model using an artificial neural network","url":"https://scielo.org.za/scielo.php?script=sci_arttext&pid=S2225-62532019000200013"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting | PLOS One","url":"https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0082056"},{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"Accurate prediction of blast-induced ground vibration intensity using optimized machine learning models - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214914725002028"},{"title":"A case study of blasting vibration attenuation based on wave component characteristics - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1674775522002001"},{"title":"Attenuation model of tunnel blast vibration velocity based on the influence of free surface","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC8548544/"}]},"I found relevant research on blast vibration prediction models. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Empirical Models for Blast Vibration Prediction\n\nRecent research has established empirical prediction equations for blasting vibration propagation and attenuation, with separate equations for PPV (peak particle velocity, resultant velocity) and PPVi (maximum value of the three component velocities; i = x, y, z represent peak component particle velocity).\n\n## Site-Specific Parameters\n\nSite-specific constants k and β describe the characteristic of propagating media, with λ representing the inelastic attenuation factor of the site, and n representing the energy diminishing influenced by rock properties and geological discontinuities (e.g., faults, fractures, joints, fissures, and bedding planes). With the increase of the GSI (Geological Strength Index) of the surrounding rock mass, the site coefficients β and k of the blasting vibration prediction equation show a decreasing trend as a whole.\n\n## Component Attenuation Considerations\n\nA typical blasting vibration wave is a composite wave, and its attenuation law is affected by the type of dominant wave component. Researchers have established attenuation equations of peak particle velocity taking into account the attenuation characteristics of P-, S- and R-waves in the blasting vibration wave.\n\n## Improving Model Accuracy\n\nBy using site-specific structural discontinuities as input, model performance can be further improved."],"durationSeconds":7.290272583999991,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YURo4oCUdBa87RHtk9gBLS","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast induced vibration waveform \"amplitude scaling\" \"recorded\" \"peak\" predict ground motion engineering\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Determination of blast induced ground vibration equations for rocks using mechanical and geological properties\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/298484361_Determination_of_blast_induced_ground_vibration_equations_for_rocks_using_mechanical_and_geological_properties\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of ground vibration level induced by blasting at different rock units - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160911000670\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting\"},{\"title\":\"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Comparison Between Empirical and Artificial-Neural-Network Approaches\",\"url\":\"https://uknowledge.uky.edu/mng_etds/75/\"},{\"title\":\"Advancing the Prediction and Evaluation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Deep Ensemble Learning with Uncertainty Assessment\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/5/182\"},{\"title\":\"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"}]\n\nI've found relevant research on blast-induced vibration waveforms and ground motion prediction. Here's what the search results reveal:\n\n## Key Findings on Blast-Induced Vibration Prediction\n\n**Amplitude Scaling and Peak Particle Velocity:**\n\nThe USBM square root scaling model is widely used in vibration prediction studies to relate Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) with scaled distance, as it effectively captures attenuation behavior in most blasting environments. This scaling approach is fundamental to predicting ground motion in engineering applications.\n\n**Recording and Waveform Analysis:**\n\nFull vibration waveforms from signature holes were recorded with several seismographs at distances ranging from 20 to 100 meters from the signature holes. With the signature hole data as the input to the model, the model prediction agrees well with field measurements of PVS of particle velocity and amplitude spectrum from production blasts.\n\n**Ground Motion Recording:**\n\nThe ground vibration is generally recorded based on two factors: Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and frequency. Additionally, Scaled distance and peak particle velocity data pairs were carefully recorded and analyzed statistically, allowing the documentation of empirical relationships between scale distance and peak particle velocity in different surface and underground regions.\n\n**Prediction Models:**\n\nThe assessment of vibrations produced by blasting has traditionally relied on maximum charge weight per delay scaling laws, with two-parameter or three-parameter models that depend on a curve fit to measured data.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"70ddae45-6cb2-4ff4-a719-3e9d19711f12","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:24:59.261Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast induced vibration waveform \"amplitude scaling\" \"recorded\" \"peak\" predict ground motion engineering","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0157Y16zoGYh1ppE1fsQuFFZ","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Determination of blast induced ground vibration equations for rocks using mechanical and geological properties","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/298484361_Determination_of_blast_induced_ground_vibration_equations_for_rocks_using_mechanical_and_geological_properties"},{"title":"Prediction of ground vibration level induced by blasting at different rock units - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160911000670"},{"title":"(PDF) Modified scaled distance regression analysis approach for prediction of blast-induced ground vibration in multi-hole blasting","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/328607748_Modified_scaled_distance_regression_analysis_approach_for_prediction_of_blast-induced_ground_vibration_in_multi-hole_blasting"},{"title":"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888"},{"title":"Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Comparison Between Empirical and Artificial-Neural-Network Approaches","url":"https://uknowledge.uky.edu/mng_etds/75/"},{"title":"Advancing the Prediction and Evaluation of Blast-Induced Ground Vibration Using Deep Ensemble Learning with Uncertainty Assessment","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2076-3263/15/5/182"},{"title":"Predictions of Experimentally Observed Stochastic Ground Vibrations Induced by Blasting","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC3866117/"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"}]},"I've found relevant research on blast-induced vibration waveforms and ground motion prediction. Here's what the search results reveal:\n\n## Key Findings on Blast-Induced Vibration Prediction\n\n**Amplitude Scaling and Peak Particle Velocity:**\n\nThe USBM square root scaling model is widely used in vibration prediction studies to relate Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) with scaled distance, as it effectively captures attenuation behavior in most blasting environments. This scaling approach is fundamental to predicting ground motion in engineering applications.\n\n**Recording and Waveform Analysis:**\n\nFull vibration waveforms from signature holes were recorded with several seismographs at distances ranging from 20 to 100 meters from the signature holes. With the signature hole data as the input to the model, the model prediction agrees well with field measurements of PVS of particle velocity and amplitude spectrum from production blasts.\n\n**Ground Motion Recording:**\n\nThe ground vibration is generally recorded based on two factors: Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and frequency. Additionally, Scaled distance and peak particle velocity data pairs were carefully recorded and analyzed statistically, allowing the documentation of empirical relationships between scale distance and peak particle velocity in different surface and underground regions.\n\n**Prediction Models:**\n\nThe assessment of vibrations produced by blasting has traditionally relied on maximum charge weight per delay scaling laws, with two-parameter or three-parameter models that depend on a curve fit to measured data."],"durationSeconds":10.507894500000024,"searchCount":1}}
