{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01SnRC7Cwjri5Ga44GnGGUSw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FDW1tdJGWVMik9mkBsNJXx","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"blast vibration attenuation \"peak acceleration\" PPA scaled distance empirical equation site coefficients"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":784,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1299,"cache_read_input_tokens":70738,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1299,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":23,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"e90f28a8-a24e-411f-8dff-fc1b399eb1f8","request_id":"req_011Ccm7eaUFjWnxiMFD8vPwT"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XQsWsumQSwLMf4KomZ7QZ7","type":"tool_result","content":"# Signature Hole Waveform Analysis: Component Treatment and Scaling\n\nBased on the provided PDF content, here are the key findings:\n\n## Component Waveform Treatment\n\nThe document addresses three orthogonal motion components in blast vibration monitoring:\n- **Transverse** (perpendicular to blast direction)\n- **Vertical** (up-down motion)\n- **Longitudinal** (along blast direction)\n\nThese are treated as independent measurements, each recorded separately to characterize full ground motion response.\n\n## Amplitude Scaling by Charge Weight\n\nThe signature hole methodology incorporates charge normalization. Rather than comparing absolute vibration levels, the analysis scales waveform amplitudes relative to explosive quantity. This normalized approach enables comparison across different blasting scenarios by removing the dominant effect of charge magnitude on motion intensity.\n\n## Limitations of Peak-Based Scaling\n\nA critical constraint emerges: relying on peak amplitude values for normalization \"preserves the dominant frequency response but may not fully capture transient behavior or phase relationships\" between components. Peak-based methods risk losing:\n- **Frequency content nuances** across the full spectrum\n- **Phase coherence** between orthogonal directions\n- **Transient characteristics** that peak values alone cannot represent\n\nThis suggests practitioners should complement peak scaling with frequency-domain or time-domain analyses examining complete waveform characteristics rather than isolated maximum values.\n\n[Binary content (application/pdf, 129.3KB) also saved to /Users/averrik/.claude/projects/-Users-averrik-Cloud-github-projects-AR-S2J2J-R-TITO-sessions-run-20260706-132143/571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63/tool-results/webfetch-1783355131229-nummop.pdf]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"c808f951-1784-4973-974e-4d727fceaf4a","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:25:38.298Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":132404,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Signature Hole Waveform Analysis: Component Treatment and Scaling\n\nBased on the provided PDF content, here are the key findings:\n\n## Component Waveform Treatment\n\nThe document addresses three orthogonal motion components in blast vibration monitoring:\n- **Transverse** (perpendicular to blast direction)\n- **Vertical** (up-down motion)\n- **Longitudinal** (along blast direction)\n\nThese are treated as independent measurements, each recorded separately to characterize full ground motion response.\n\n## Amplitude Scaling by Charge Weight\n\nThe signature hole methodology incorporates charge normalization. Rather than comparing absolute vibration levels, the analysis scales waveform amplitudes relative to explosive quantity. This normalized approach enables comparison across different blasting scenarios by removing the dominant effect of charge magnitude on motion intensity.\n\n## Limitations of Peak-Based Scaling\n\nA critical constraint emerges: relying on peak amplitude values for normalization \"preserves the dominant frequency response but may not fully capture transient behavior or phase relationships\" between components. Peak-based methods risk losing:\n- **Frequency content nuances** across the full spectrum\n- **Phase coherence** between orthogonal directions\n- **Transient characteristics** that peak values alone cannot represent\n\nThis suggests practitioners should complement peak scaling with frequency-domain or time-domain analyses examining complete waveform characteristics rather than isolated maximum values.\n\n[Binary content (application/pdf, 129.3KB) also saved to /Users/averrik/.claude/projects/-Users-averrik-Cloud-github-projects-AR-S2J2J-R-TITO-sessions-run-20260706-132143/571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63/tool-results/webfetch-1783355131229-nummop.pdf]","durationMs":9414,"url":"https://miningandblasting.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2009/09/signature-hole-blast-vibration-control-e28093-twenty-years-hence-and-beyond.pdf"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FDW1tdJGWVMik9mkBsNJXx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration attenuation \"peak acceleration\" PPA scaled distance empirical equation site coefficients\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/323226586_Empirical_and_probabilistic_analysis_of_blast-induced_ground_vibrations\"},{\"title\":\"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Theoretical Derivation of a Peak Particle Velocity–Distance Law for the Prediction of Vibrations from Blasting\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225463499_Theoretical_Derivation_of_a_Peak_Particle_Velocity-Distance_Law_for_the_Prediction_of_Vibrations_from_Blasting\"},{\"title\":\"Propagation characteristics and prediction of blast-induced vibration on closely spaced rock tunnels - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0886779822000566\"},{\"title\":\"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Expansion of Blast Vibration Attenuation Equations for Deeply Buried Small Clearance Tunnels Based on Dimensional Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.889504/full\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration attenuation with peak acceleration and scaled distance empirical equations:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Scaled Distance Method**: The most widely adopted equation for predicting peak particle velocity (PPV) is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, where scaled distance is the ratio of the distance of the monitoring point from the geometric centre of the blasting area (D) to the maximum charge per delay.\n\n**Site Coefficients**: Site-specific constants k and β describe the characteristics of the propagating media, blasting design and geology. Additionally, the site coefficient k is generally within 3,000.\n\n**Vibration Attenuation Factors**: The attenuation of ground motion depends mainly on the charge weight, frequency content of wave motion, and the elastic properties of the transmitting medium.\n\n**Peak Acceleration**: Peak particle acceleration (PPA), velocity (PPV), and displacement (PPD) are used to characterize ground motion from blasting.\n\n**Attenuation Equations**: Empirical prediction equations of blasting vibration propagation and attenuation were established for PPV and for maximum values of the three component velocities.\n\nThe search results indicate that empirical equations using scaled distance with site-specific coefficients are the standard approach for predicting blast-induced ground vibrations, though the results emphasize that various site-dependent factors influence the accuracy of these predictions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"7106a860-4291-4907-8c7e-aa5415a88123","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:25:38.759Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration attenuation \"peak acceleration\" PPA scaled distance empirical equation site coefficients","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_017zQJJTc5NJWkuiiPpXeBmm","content":[{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations | Request PDF","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/323226586_Empirical_and_probabilistic_analysis_of_blast-induced_ground_vibrations"},{"title":"Attenuation assessment of blast-induced vibrations derived from an underground mine - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160919307592"},{"title":"(PDF) Theoretical Derivation of a Peak Particle Velocity–Distance Law for the Prediction of Vibrations from Blasting","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/225463499_Theoretical_Derivation_of_a_Peak_Particle_Velocity-Distance_Law_for_the_Prediction_of_Vibrations_from_Blasting"},{"title":"Propagation characteristics and prediction of blast-induced vibration on closely spaced rock tunnels - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0886779822000566"},{"title":"Empirical and probabilistic analysis of blast-induced ground vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160917305725"},{"title":"Frontiers | Expansion of Blast Vibration Attenuation Equations for Deeply Buried Small Clearance Tunnels Based on Dimensional Analysis","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.889504/full"},{"title":"Prediction of peak particle velocity using hybrid random forest approach","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC11680976/"},{"title":"Evaluation of underground blast-induced ground motions through near-surface low-velocity geological layers - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S167477552200172X"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration attenuation with peak acceleration and scaled distance empirical equations:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Scaled Distance Method**: The most widely adopted equation for predicting peak particle velocity (PPV) is the scaled distance (SD) relationship, where scaled distance is the ratio of the distance of the monitoring point from the geometric centre of the blasting area (D) to the maximum charge per delay.\n\n**Site Coefficients**: Site-specific constants k and β describe the characteristics of the propagating media, blasting design and geology. Additionally, the site coefficient k is generally within 3,000.\n\n**Vibration Attenuation Factors**: The attenuation of ground motion depends mainly on the charge weight, frequency content of wave motion, and the elastic properties of the transmitting medium.\n\n**Peak Acceleration**: Peak particle acceleration (PPA), velocity (PPV), and displacement (PPD) are used to characterize ground motion from blasting.\n\n**Attenuation Equations**: Empirical prediction equations of blasting vibration propagation and attenuation were established for PPV and for maximum values of the three component velocities.\n\nThe search results indicate that empirical equations using scaled distance with site-specific coefficients are the standard approach for predicting blast-induced ground vibrations, though the results emphasize that various site-dependent factors influence the accuracy of these predictions."],"durationSeconds":7.714869957999996,"searchCount":1}}
