{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CgReixxpvwZXTEiyhs9wpo","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration \"frequency content\" \"phase\" limitations amplitude-only scaling waveform\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017\"},{\"title\":\"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888\"},{\"title\":\"Method of reducing ground vibration from delay blasting\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/5388521\"},{\"title\":\"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023\"},{\"title\":\"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160913001822\"},{\"title\":\"Ground Vibration Monitoring Standards and Methodology\",\"url\":\"https://groundvibrationmonitoring.com/ground-vibration-monitoring-standards-methodology/\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis of blasting vibration duration considering frequency and energy and its application - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11253058/\"},{\"title\":\"Experimental and theoretical analysis of charge length on single-hole vibration amplitude from underground deep-hole blasting - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160924002417\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Frequency spectrum and energy refinement characteristics of blasting vibration signals in raw water pipeline tunnel excavation\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.966222/full\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration frequency content, phase, and limitations of amplitude-only scaling:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Limitations of Amplitude-Only Scaling:**\n\nThe common time-domain superposition method has limitations in accurately controlling spectrum characteristics, indicating that amplitude-only approaches are insufficient for comprehensive waveform analysis.\n\n**Phase and Frequency Domain Considerations:**\n\nResearchers have proposed an amplitude-phase joint waveform simulation method based on Fourier analysis to meet the needs of blasting vibration analysis and safety assessment. This approach addresses the limitations of amplitude-only methods by incorporating phase information. The dominant frequency component of the unwrapped phase spectrum is linear, and its slope has a logarithmic function relationship with the proportional distance.\n\n**Magnitude and Phase Variation with Distance:**\n\nThe variation of magnitude spectrum and phase spectrum with the propagation distance establishes the quantitative expression and calculation flow of the magnitude-phase joint waveform simulation method in the frequency domain.\n\n**Frequency Content Behavior:**\n\nThe amplitudes of particle velocities and frequency contents are higher at the near-field record station and they are decreased at far-field locations, demonstrating that both amplitude and frequency characteristics change with distance.\n\n**Waveform Analysis Importance:**\n\nFull waveform studies provide complete information on blast vibration analysis, including: (1) PPV, (2) The blast-frequencies stimulating soil resonance frequencies (wave interferences), (3) Duration of the blast vibrations.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"15ca3695-c13f-41d1-8ba5-95025ffe97df","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:25:56.832Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration \"frequency content\" \"phase\" limitations amplitude-only scaling waveform","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_015HfHninPT5gpByUdPKE8qE","content":[{"title":"Simulation of Blasting Vibration Waveform based on Magnitude-phase Variation Law in Frequency Domain","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2025.04.017"},{"title":"Waveform analysis in mitigation of blast-induced vibrations - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0926985108000888"},{"title":"Method of reducing ground vibration from delay blasting","url":"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/5388521"},{"title":"Research Review on Blast Vibration Intensity, Waveform and Spectrum: Prediction and Active Control","url":"https://www.sciopen.com/article/10.3963/j.issn.1001-487X.2024.03.023"},{"title":"Blast vibration dependence on charge length, velocity of detonation and layered media - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160913001822"},{"title":"Ground Vibration Monitoring Standards and Methodology","url":"https://groundvibrationmonitoring.com/ground-vibration-monitoring-standards-methodology/"},{"title":"Analysis of blasting vibration duration considering frequency and energy and its application - PMC","url":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11253058/"},{"title":"Experimental and theoretical analysis of charge length on single-hole vibration amplitude from underground deep-hole blasting - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1365160924002417"},{"title":"Frontiers | Frequency spectrum and energy refinement characteristics of blasting vibration signals in raw water pipeline tunnel excavation","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/earth-science/articles/10.3389/feart.2022.966222/full"}]},"Based on the search results, here's what I found regarding blast vibration frequency content, phase, and limitations of amplitude-only scaling:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Limitations of Amplitude-Only Scaling:**\n\nThe common time-domain superposition method has limitations in accurately controlling spectrum characteristics, indicating that amplitude-only approaches are insufficient for comprehensive waveform analysis.\n\n**Phase and Frequency Domain Considerations:**\n\nResearchers have proposed an amplitude-phase joint waveform simulation method based on Fourier analysis to meet the needs of blasting vibration analysis and safety assessment. This approach addresses the limitations of amplitude-only methods by incorporating phase information. The dominant frequency component of the unwrapped phase spectrum is linear, and its slope has a logarithmic function relationship with the proportional distance.\n\n**Magnitude and Phase Variation with Distance:**\n\nThe variation of magnitude spectrum and phase spectrum with the propagation distance establishes the quantitative expression and calculation flow of the magnitude-phase joint waveform simulation method in the frequency domain.\n\n**Frequency Content Behavior:**\n\nThe amplitudes of particle velocities and frequency contents are higher at the near-field record station and they are decreased at far-field locations, demonstrating that both amplitude and frequency characteristics change with distance.\n\n**Waveform Analysis Importance:**\n\nFull waveform studies provide complete information on blast vibration analysis, including: (1) PPV, (2) The blast-frequencies stimulating soil resonance frequencies (wave interferences), (3) Duration of the blast vibrations."],"durationSeconds":7.722349791999994,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015MbaGUQLU2coMRoJYq1GCx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"blast vibration \"three-component\" OR \"triaxial\" recorded waveform scaling acceleration structural analysis input\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"(PDF) Using blast vibration measurements to estimate rock triaxial strains/stresses and dynamic rock strength for blast damage evaluation\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/289491123_Using_blast_vibration_measurements_to_estimate_rock_triaxial_strainsstresses_and_dynamic_rock_strength_for_blast_damage_evaluation\"},{\"title\":\"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849\"},{\"title\":\"Analysis of vibration signals near ground surface during blasting excavation of a tunnel in fractured rock | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-73089-1\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Analysis of blasting vibration wave propagation based on finite element numerical calculation and experimental investigations\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318074546_Analysis_of_blasting_vibration_wave_propagation_based_on_finite_element_numerical_calculation_and_experimental_investigations\"},{\"title\":\"Quarry Blast Vibration Monitoring in a Family Home\",\"url\":\"https://dewesoft.com/blog/monitoring-quarry-vibrations-in-house\"},{\"title\":\"Motion parameter estimation and measured data correction derived from blast-induced vibration: New insights - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0263224118311047\"},{\"title\":\"Basics of Structural Vibration Testing and Analysis\",\"url\":\"https://www.crystalinstruments.com/basics-of-structural-vibration-testing-and-analysis\"},{\"title\":\"Blasting | Instantel\",\"url\":\"https://www.instantel.com/applications/blasting\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here's what I found on blast vibration analysis using three-component/triaxial recorded waveforms and their application to structural analysis:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Three-Component/Triaxial Recording Methods:**\n\nGround motions are recorded using triaxial geophones combined with surface strain measurements on load-bearing walls, enabling reconstruction of three-dimensional dynamic stress states within structures. Additionally, triaxial acceleration devices are used to measure ground acceleration, and triaxial MEMS accelerometers combined with ADC converters are employed for blast vibration monitoring.\n\n**Waveform Characteristics:**\n\nThe surface experiences triaxial vibrations resulting from the superposition of vibrations induced by individual sections of the blast, showing multiple vibration peaks. Research has shown directional variations, where along the x-direction, the vibration peak was highest, followed by the y-direction, while the z-direction exhibited the smallest peak.\n\n**Structural Analysis Input:**\n\nBlast-induced ground vibrations are typically evaluated using Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and frequency-band criteria; however, these descriptors provide only an indirect indication of the stress demand experienced by structures. Acceleration can directly reflect the inertial force and dynamic response of structures and buildings, while particle velocity is the most common descriptor and directly correlates with damage and energy variation.\n\n**Signal Analysis Approaches:**\n\nSignal interpretation employs one-third-octave analysis, Matching Pursuit time-frequency-energy decomposition, and response spectrum analysis.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"7ad9ffd4-717b-4521-a7de-2bcc7c08518c","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:25:56.995Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"blast vibration \"three-component\" OR \"triaxial\" recorded waveform scaling acceleration structural analysis input","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_014SQBMAcvPKvq2MXCpHX4rj","content":[{"title":"(PDF) Using blast vibration measurements to estimate rock triaxial strains/stresses and dynamic rock strength for blast damage evaluation","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/289491123_Using_blast_vibration_measurements_to_estimate_rock_triaxial_strainsstresses_and_dynamic_rock_strength_for_blast_damage_evaluation"},{"title":"Strain-based assessment of nominally high-frequency blast induced vibrations in a near-field masonry housing structure - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0141029626008849"},{"title":"Analysis of vibration signals near ground surface during blasting excavation of a tunnel in fractured rock | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-024-73089-1"},{"title":"(PDF) Analysis of blasting vibration wave propagation based on finite element numerical calculation and experimental investigations","url":"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/318074546_Analysis_of_blasting_vibration_wave_propagation_based_on_finite_element_numerical_calculation_and_experimental_investigations"},{"title":"Quarry Blast Vibration Monitoring in a Family Home","url":"https://dewesoft.com/blog/monitoring-quarry-vibrations-in-house"},{"title":"Motion parameter estimation and measured data correction derived from blast-induced vibration: New insights - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0263224118311047"},{"title":"Basics of Structural Vibration Testing and Analysis","url":"https://www.crystalinstruments.com/basics-of-structural-vibration-testing-and-analysis"},{"title":"Blasting | Instantel","url":"https://www.instantel.com/applications/blasting"}]},"Based on the web search results, here's what I found on blast vibration analysis using three-component/triaxial recorded waveforms and their application to structural analysis:\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Three-Component/Triaxial Recording Methods:**\n\nGround motions are recorded using triaxial geophones combined with surface strain measurements on load-bearing walls, enabling reconstruction of three-dimensional dynamic stress states within structures. Additionally, triaxial acceleration devices are used to measure ground acceleration, and triaxial MEMS accelerometers combined with ADC converters are employed for blast vibration monitoring.\n\n**Waveform Characteristics:**\n\nThe surface experiences triaxial vibrations resulting from the superposition of vibrations induced by individual sections of the blast, showing multiple vibration peaks. Research has shown directional variations, where along the x-direction, the vibration peak was highest, followed by the y-direction, while the z-direction exhibited the smallest peak.\n\n**Structural Analysis Input:**\n\nBlast-induced ground vibrations are typically evaluated using Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and frequency-band criteria; however, these descriptors provide only an indirect indication of the stress demand experienced by structures. Acceleration can directly reflect the inertial force and dynamic response of structures and buildings, while particle velocity is the most common descriptor and directly correlates with damage and energy variation.\n\n**Signal Analysis Approaches:**\n\nSignal interpretation employs one-third-octave analysis, Matching Pursuit time-frequency-energy decomposition, and response spectrum analysis."],"durationSeconds":8.37408587499999,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014Q32QBrUXEPa3UDV443iaj","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ground vibration prediction surface regression PPV PPA \"component\" simultaneous model prediction surface\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Simultaneous prediction and optimisation of rock fragmentation and ground vibration using an ANN–RF ensemble in open-pit blasting | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-33871-1\"},{\"title\":\"Data-driven machine learning approaches for simultaneous prediction of peak particle velocity and frequency induced by rock blasting in mining - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2773230424000659\"},{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Intelligent ground vibration prediction in surface mines using an efficient soft computing method based on field data\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.1094771/full\"},{\"title\":\"Evaluation and Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) Approach\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-6489/3/4/36\"},{\"title\":\"Artificial neural network and regression models for prediction of free-field ground vibration parameters induced from vibroflotation - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726121002451\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of ground vibration due to mine blasting in a surface lead–zinc mine using machine learning ensemble techniques\",\"url\":\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10121721/\"},{\"title\":\"Prediction of ground vibration due to mine blasting in a surface lead–zinc mine using machine learning ensemble techniques | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-33796-7\"},{\"title\":\"Accurate prediction of blast-induced ground vibration intensity using optimized machine learning models - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214914725002028\"},{\"title\":\"A multi-parameters assessment method and prediction model for ground vibration under the high-speed railway bridge | Scientific Reports\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-93595-0\"}]\n\nI found relevant research on ground vibration prediction using various surface regression and machine learning approaches. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Simultaneous Prediction Models\n\nResearch uses ensemble machine learning approaches to simultaneously predict and optimize rock fragmentation and ground vibration during blasting operations, modeling a solution surface. The ANN-RF ensemble achieved strong performance metrics including R² = 0.930, RMSE = 0.380, MAE = 0.302 for PPV prediction.\n\n## Hybrid Regression Approaches\n\nA hybrid soft computing approach called RSM-SVR comprises response surface model (RSM) and support vector regression (SVR), structured using RSM in the first calibrating process and SVR in the second to improve accuracy of blast-induced PPV predictions.\n\n## PPV and PPA Prediction\n\nAssessment of blasting vibrations requires evaluation of Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and frequency as essential parameters for measuring vibration velocity. Ground vibration assessment can be conducted using multiple parameters including PPV, PPA, and VLZ to evaluate different effects on building structures, human distress, and environmental suitability.\n\n## Alternative Prediction Models\n\nDifferent soft computing techniques including Gaussian process regression (GPR), decision tree (DT), and support vector regression (SVR) are applied for blast-induced ground vibration prediction. The CatBoost regression model demonstrates superior performance in predicting both PPV and frequency.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"571dab6f-93f8-46f1-b7c5-b688e09d8b63","uuid":"7f2b138c-88c0-4b59-8849-e2c5f1841a5b","timestamp":"2026-07-06T16:25:57.456Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ground vibration prediction surface regression PPV PPA \"component\" simultaneous model prediction surface","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_0196GeU96QzhJ2LqPMHFYx5n","content":[{"title":"Simultaneous prediction and optimisation of rock fragmentation and ground vibration using an ANN–RF ensemble in open-pit blasting | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-33871-1"},{"title":"Data-driven machine learning approaches for simultaneous prediction of peak particle velocity and frequency induced by rock blasting in mining - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2773230424000659"},{"title":"Frontiers | Intelligent ground vibration prediction in surface mines using an efficient soft computing method based on field data","url":"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2022.1094771/full"},{"title":"Evaluation and Prediction of Blast-Induced Ground Vibrations: A Gaussian Process Regression (GPR) Approach","url":"https://www.mdpi.com/2673-6489/3/4/36"},{"title":"Artificial neural network and regression models for prediction of free-field ground vibration parameters induced from vibroflotation - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0267726121002451"},{"title":"Prediction of ground vibration due to mine blasting in a surface lead–zinc mine using machine learning ensemble techniques","url":"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC10121721/"},{"title":"Prediction of ground vibration due to mine blasting in a surface lead–zinc mine using machine learning ensemble techniques | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-023-33796-7"},{"title":"Accurate prediction of blast-induced ground vibration intensity using optimized machine learning models - ScienceDirect","url":"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2214914725002028"},{"title":"A multi-parameters assessment method and prediction model for ground vibration under the high-speed railway bridge | Scientific Reports","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-93595-0"}]},"I found relevant research on ground vibration prediction using various surface regression and machine learning approaches. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Simultaneous Prediction Models\n\nResearch uses ensemble machine learning approaches to simultaneously predict and optimize rock fragmentation and ground vibration during blasting operations, modeling a solution surface. The ANN-RF ensemble achieved strong performance metrics including R² = 0.930, RMSE = 0.380, MAE = 0.302 for PPV prediction.\n\n## Hybrid Regression Approaches\n\nA hybrid soft computing approach called RSM-SVR comprises response surface model (RSM) and support vector regression (SVR), structured using RSM in the first calibrating process and SVR in the second to improve accuracy of blast-induced PPV predictions.\n\n## PPV and PPA Prediction\n\nAssessment of blasting vibrations requires evaluation of Peak Particle Velocity (PPV) and frequency as essential parameters for measuring vibration velocity. Ground vibration assessment can be conducted using multiple parameters including PPV, PPA, and VLZ to evaluate different effects on building structures, human distress, and environmental suitability.\n\n## Alternative Prediction Models\n\nDifferent soft computing techniques including Gaussian process regression (GPR), decision tree (DT), and support vector regression (SVR) are applied for blast-induced ground vibration prediction. The CatBoost regression model demonstrates superior performance in predicting both PPV and frequency."],"durationSeconds":7.696504832999985,"searchCount":1}}
